self_contained_problem:
Let $V = \mathbb{C}^4 \otimes \mathbb{C}^4 \otimes \mathbb{C}^4$, identified with the space of $4\times 4\times 4$ complex arrays $(p_{ijk})_{i,j,k\in\{1,2,3,4\}}$. Define the variety $X \subseteq V$ to be the Zariski closure of the set of tensors of rank at most $4$, i.e. those admitting a decomposition
$$p_{ijk} = \sum_{r=1}^{4} a^{(r)}_i\, b^{(r)}_j\, c^{(r)}_k$$
for some vectors $a^{(r)}, b^{(r)}, c^{(r)} \in \mathbb{C}^4$. Consider the following families of polynomials, each known to vanish identically on $X$:
(i) the degree-$5$ Strassen commutativity polynomials, obtained from the condition that, for any choice of two parallel slices of the tensor, suitable $3\times 3$ minor relations vanish;
(ii) the degree-$6$ Landsberg–Manivel polynomials arising from $\mathrm{GL}(4)^{\times 3}$-representation theory;
(iii) the degree-$9$ symmetrization polynomials.
Let $I_9 \subseteq \mathbb{C}[p_{ijk}]$ be the ideal generated by all such polynomials of degree at most $9$. Question: does the zero locus $V(I_9) \subseteq V$ equal $X$ set-theoretically?

Let $\mathrm{PD}_n$ denote the cone of real symmetric positive definite $n\times n$ matrices. For a matrix $\Sigma=(\sigma_{ij})\in\mathrm{PD}_n$, indices $i\neq j$ in $[n]:=\{1,\dots,n\}$, and a subset $K\subseteq [n]\setminus\{i,j\}$, the almost-principal minor $A_{ij|K}(\Sigma)$ is the determinant of the submatrix of $\Sigma$ with row index set $\{i\}\cup K$ and column index set $\{j\}\cup K$. Write $\mathcal{A}_n=\{(ij|K):i,j\in[n],\,i\neq j,\,K\subseteq[n]\setminus\{i,j\}\}$ (with $(ij|K)$ identified with $(ji|K)$).

A subset $G\subseteq\mathcal{A}_n$ is called a gaussoid if, for every relabeling of $[n]$ and every operation corresponding to taking Schur complements, $G$ satisfies the following four axioms whenever the indices involved are distinct elements of $[n]$ and $L\subseteq[n]\setminus\{i,j,k\}$:
(a) if $(ij|L)\in G$ and $(ik|L)\in G$, then $(ij|L\cup\{k\})\in G$ and $(ik|L\cup\{j\})\in G$;
(b) if $(ij|L)\in G$ and $(ij|L\cup\{k\})\in G$, then $(ik|L)\in G$ or $(jk|L)\in G$;
(c) if $(ij|L\cup\{k\})\in G$ and $(ik|L\cup\{j\})\in G$, then $(ij|L)\in G$ and $(ik|L)\in G$;
(d) if $(ij|L)\in G$ and $(ik|L\cup\{j\})\in G$, then $(ij|L\cup\{k\})\in G$ and $(ik|L)\in G$.

A gaussoid $G\subseteq\mathcal{A}_n$ is called representable if there exists $\Sigma\in\mathrm{PD}_n$ such that
$$G=\{(ij|K)\in\mathcal{A}_n:A_{ij|K}(\Sigma)=0\}.$$

Problem. For $n=5$, classify all representable gaussoids on $[5]$ (equivalently, determine which gaussoids on $\mathcal{A}_5$ arise as the set of vanishing almost-principal minors of some matrix in $\mathrm{PD}_5$).

Let $A$ and $B$ be semisimple unital complex Banach algebras. Recall that an element $a$ of a unital Banach algebra is invertible if there exists $b$ in the algebra with $ab = ba = 1$. The Jordan product on a Banach algebra is defined by $x \circ y = \tfrac{1}{2}(xy + yx)$. A linear map $T : A \to B$ is called:
- unital if $T(1_A) = 1_B$;
- invertibility-preserving if $T(a)$ is invertible in $B$ whenever $a$ is invertible in $A$;
- a Jordan epimorphism if it is surjective and satisfies $T(x \circ y) = T(x) \circ T(y)$ for all $x,y \in A$.

Question: Suppose $T : A \to B$ is a unital, surjective, invertibility-preserving linear map. Must $T$ necessarily be a Jordan epimorphism?

Let $k$ be a field and let $n > 2$ be an integer. Consider the polynomial ring $k[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ and the group $\mathrm{GA}_n(k)$ of polynomial automorphisms of $\mathbb{A}^n_k$, i.e., $k$-algebra automorphisms of $k[x_1,\dots,x_n]$. Inside $\mathrm{GA}_n(k)$, define:
\begin{itemize}
\item the affine subgroup $\mathrm{Aff}_n(k)$, consisting of automorphisms of the form $x_i \mapsto \sum_j a_{ij} x_j + b_i$ with $(a_{ij}) \in \mathrm{GL}_n(k)$ and $b_i \in k$;
\item the triangular (de Jonquières) subgroup $\mathrm{BA}_n(k)$, consisting of automorphisms of the form $x_i \mapsto c_i x_i + f_i(x_{i+1},\dots,x_n)$ with $c_i \in k^{\times}$ and $f_i \in k[x_{i+1},\dots,x_n]$;
\item the tame subgroup $\mathrm{TA}_n(k) := \langle \mathrm{Aff}_n(k), \mathrm{BA}_n(k)\rangle$.
\end{itemize}
Determine whether $\mathrm{Aff}_n(k)$ is a maximal subgroup of $\mathrm{TA}_n(k)$; that is, whether every subgroup $H$ with $\mathrm{Aff}_n(k) \subseteq H \subseteq \mathrm{TA}_n(k)$ satisfies $H = \mathrm{Aff}_n(k)$ or $H = \mathrm{TA}_n(k)$.

Let $p>5$ be a prime. Define
$$S_p \;=\; \sum_{n=0}^{p-1} \frac{357n+103}{2160^{n}} \binom{2n}{n} \sum_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k}\binom{n+2k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k}(-324)^{n-k},$$
$$T_p \;=\; \sum_{n=0}^{p-1} \frac{1}{2160^{n}} \binom{2n}{n} \sum_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k}\binom{n+2k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k}(-324)^{n-k}.$$
Let $\left(\tfrac{\cdot}{p}\right)$ denote the Legendre symbol. Prove or disprove the following two assertions.

(A) One has
$$S_p \;\equiv\; p\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)\!\left(54 + 49\left(\tfrac{\cdot}{p}\right)\right) \pmod{p^2},$$
where the symbol $\left(\tfrac{\cdot}{p}\right)$ on the right is to be interpreted as the appropriate quadratic character (so that the right-hand side is a well-defined residue mod $p^2$).

(B) Depending on the values of $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)$, one has the following congruences mod $p^2$:
- if all four symbols are $1$ and $p=x^2+105y^2$ with $x,y\in\mathbb{Z}$, then $T_p\equiv 4x^2-2p$;
- if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=-1$ and $2p=x^2+105y^2$, then $T_p\equiv 2x^2-2p$;
- if all four symbols are $-1$ and $p=3x^2+35y^2$, then $T_p\equiv 2p-12x^2$;
- if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=-1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=1$ and $2p=3x^2+35y^2$, then $T_p\equiv 2p-6x^2$;
- if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=-1$ and $p=5x^2+21y^2$, then $T_p\equiv 20x^2-2p$;
- if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=-1$ and $2p=5x^2+21y^2$, then $T_p\equiv 10x^2-2p$;
- if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=-1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=1$ and $p=7x^2+15y^2$, then $T_p\equiv 28x^2-2p$;
- if $\left(\tfrac{-1}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=-1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=1$ and $2p=7x^2+15y^2$, then $T_p\equiv 14x^2-2p$;
- if $\left(\tfrac{-105}{p}\right)=-1$, then $T_p\equiv 0$.

In addition, prove the Ramanujan-type series
$$\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{357n+103}{2160^{n}} \binom{2n}{n} \sum_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k}\binom{n+2k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k}(-324)^{n-k} \;=\; \frac{90}{\pi}.$$

For integers $b$ and $c$ and a nonnegative integer $n$, define the generalized central trinomial coefficient $T_n(b,c)$ as the coefficient of $x^n$ in the expansion of $(x^2+bx+c)^n$; equivalently,
$$T_n(b,c)=\sum_{k=0}^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}\binom{n}{2k}\binom{2k}{k}b^{n-2k}c^k.$$
For an odd prime $q$ and an integer $a$ coprime to $q$, let $\left(\tfrac{a}{q}\right)$ denote the Legendre symbol.

Let $p>3$ be a prime. Prove or disprove the following two assertions.

(I) $\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\frac{\binom{2k}{k}^2 T_{2k}(18,1)}{256^k}$ is congruent modulo $p^2$ to:
- $4x^2-2p$, if $\left(\tfrac{2}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=1$ and $p=x^2+30y^2$;
- $12x^2-2p$, if $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{2}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=-1$ and $p=3x^2+10y^2$;
- $2p-8x^2$, if $\left(\tfrac{2}{p}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=-1$ and $p=2x^2+15y^2$;
- $2p-6x^2$, if $\left(\tfrac{p}{5}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{2}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=-1$ and $2p=3x^2+10y^2$;
- $0$, if $\left(\tfrac{-30}{p}\right)=-1$.

(II) $\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\frac{\binom{2k}{k}^2 T_{2k}(30,1)}{256^k}$ is congruent modulo $p^2$ to:
- $4x^2-2p$, if $\left(\tfrac{-2}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=1$ and $p=x^2+42y^2$;
- $12x^2-2p$, if $\left(\tfrac{-2}{p}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=-1$ and $p=3x^2+14y^2$;
- $2p-8x^2$, if $\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{-2}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=-1$ and $p=2x^2+21y^2$;
- $2p-6x^2$, if $\left(\tfrac{p}{3}\right)=1$, $\left(\tfrac{-2}{p}\right)=\left(\tfrac{p}{7}\right)=-1$ and $2p=3x^2+14y^2$;
- $0$, if $\left(\tfrac{-42}{p}\right)=-1$.

For an integer n ≥ 0 and integers b, c, define the Chebyshev-like polynomial value T_n(b,c) by the recursion T_0(b,c)=1, T_1(b,c)=b, and T_{n+1}(b,c) = b·T_n(b,c) − c·T_{n-1}(b,c); equivalently, T_n(b,c) is the coefficient sum giving T_n(b,c) = ∑_{k=0}^{⌊n/2⌋} (n choose 2k)(2k choose k)/(... ) — concretely T_n(b,c) = ∑_{k=0}^{⌊n/2⌋} \binom{n}{2k}\binom{2k}{k} b^{n-2k}(b^2-4c)^k / 2^{... }. (Equivalently, T_n(b,c) = (((b+√(b²−4c))/2)^n + ((b−√(b²−4c))/2)^n) when c=1, this is a Lucas-type sequence; in particular T_n(x,1) is the standard normalized Chebyshev value with T_n(2cosθ,1)=2cos(nθ)·… — use the recursive definition above as the working definition.)

Let p > 3 be a prime, and for an odd prime q, let (a/q) denote the Legendre symbol. Prove or disprove the following two congruences modulo p².

(I) Suppose p ∉ {13, 17}. Then
  ∑_{k=0}^{p−1} \binom{2k}{k}\binom{3k}{k} T_k(102,1) / 102^{3k}  
is congruent modulo p² to:
 • 4x² − 2p, if (2/p) = (p/3) = (p/13) = 1 and p = x² + 78y² with integers x,y;
 • 2p − 8x², if (2/p) = 1, (p/3) = (p/13) = −1 and p = 2x² + 39y²;
 • 12x² − 2p, if (p/13) = 1, (2/p) = (p/3) = −1 and p = 3x² + 26y²;
 • 2p − 24x², if (p/3) = 1, (2/p) = (p/13) = −1 and p = 6x² + 13y²;
 • 0, if (−78/p) = −1.

(II) Suppose p ∉ {11, 17}. Then
  ∑_{k=0}^{p−1} \binom{2k}{k}\binom{3k}{k} T_k(198,1) / 198^{3k}  
is congruent modulo p² to:
 • 4x² − 2p, if (2/p) = (p/3) = (p/17) = 1 and p = x² + 102y²;
 • 2p − 8x², if (p/17) = 1, (2/p) = (p/3) = −1 and p = 2x² + 51y²;
 • 12x² − 2p, if (p/3) = 1, (2/p) = (p/17) = −1 and p = 3x² + 34y²;
 • 2p − 24x², if (2/p) = 1, (p/3) = (p/17) = −1 and p = 6x² + 17y²;
 • 0, if (−102/p) = −1.

Determine whether these congruences hold for all primes p subject to the stated exclusions.

Donaldson–Thomas (DT) theory on a smooth projective Calabi–Yau threefold $X$ produces deformation-invariant integer-valued counts of coherent sheaves on $X$, defined by integrating against a virtual fundamental class on moduli spaces of stable sheaves (or ideal sheaves) of fixed Chern character, whose existence relies on the symmetric perfect obstruction theory associated with Serre duality in dimension 3.

Provide a mathematically sound framework that extends this construction to smooth projective varieties (in particular, Calabi–Yau manifolds) of complex dimension strictly greater than 3. Specifically, for $\dim_{\mathbb{C}} X > 3$, define enumerative invariants counting (semi)stable coherent sheaves, or ideal sheaves of subschemes, on $X$ that:

(a) are constructed via a virtual fundamental class on appropriate moduli spaces;
(b) are deformation invariant under deformations of the complex structure of $X$;
(c) reduce to, or naturally generalize, the standard Donaldson–Thomas invariants when $\dim_{\mathbb{C}} X = 3$;
(d) admit a sheaf-theoretic / algebro-geometric definition compatible with expected wall-crossing and generating-function structures.

The problem is to give such a definition in dimensions $> 3$ (and, in particular, to determine to what extent and in which dimensions $> 3$ such a theory can exist).

Let $F$ be an infinite field and let $n \geq 2$ be an integer. For $m \geq 2$, let $X_1, \ldots, X_m$ be $m$ generic $n \times n$ matrices, i.e., matrices whose entries $\{x_{ij}^{(k)} : 1 \le i,j \le n,\ 1 \le k \le m\}$ are algebraically independent commuting indeterminates over $F$. Let $F\{X_1,\ldots,X_m\}$ denote the $F$-subalgebra of $M_n(F(x_{ij}^{(k)}))$ generated by $X_1,\ldots,X_m$ (the ring of $m$ generic $n\times n$ matrices), and let $UD(F,m,n)$ denote its ring of central quotients (the generic division algebra). Define:
\begin{itemize}
\item $Z_m(F,n)$: the center of $UD(F,m,n)$;
\item $Z(F,n) := Z_2(F,n)$: the center of the generic division algebra on two generic $n\times n$ matrices;
\item $Z_H(F,n)$: the center of the trace ring of generic $n\times n$ matrices (the so-called Procesi/Formanek invariant field for the action of $\mathrm{PGL}_n$).
\end{itemize}
Recall that an extension $K/F$ is called:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{rational} if $K \cong F(t_1, \ldots, t_r)$ is purely transcendental over $F$;
\item \emph{stably rational} if $K(s_1, \ldots, s_\ell)$ is rational over $F$ for some independent indeterminates $s_i$;
\item \emph{retract rational} if there is an $F$-algebra inclusion $K \hookrightarrow F(t_1,\ldots,t_r)_{\mathfrak p}$ (a localization of a polynomial ring) admitting an $F$-algebra retraction onto a domain whose field of fractions is $K$.
\end{itemize}
Determine, for each $n \geq 2$ and each $m \geq 2$, whether the field extensions $Z(F,n)/F$, $Z_m(F,n)/F$, and $Z_H(F,n)/F$ are rational, stably rational, or retract rational.

Let $F$ be a field and let $G$ be an algebraic group over $F$. Recall that the essential dimension $\mathrm{ed}_F(G)$ is the smallest non-negative integer $n$ with the property that every $G$-torsor over a field extension $K/F$ is the pullback of a $G$-torsor over an intermediate subfield $F \subseteq K_0 \subseteq K$ with $\mathrm{tr.deg}_F(K_0) \le n$. For a positive integer $m$ invertible in $F$ (or, when stated, allowing $\mathrm{char}\,F$ to divide $m$), let $\mu_m \subset \mathbb{G}_m$ denote the group scheme of $m$-th roots of unity, embedded as scalar matrices in $\mathrm{GL}_n$ (when $m \mid n$). Determine the following essential dimensions:

(a) $\mathrm{ed}_F(\mathrm{GL}_8/\mu_2)$ when $\mathrm{char}\,F = 2$;

(b) $\mathrm{ed}_F(\mathrm{GL}_{16}/\mu_2)$ when $\mathrm{char}\,F \neq 2$;

(c) $\mathrm{ed}_F(\mathrm{GL}_{p^2}/\mu_p)$ for every odd prime $p$ and every field $F$.

Let F be a field and let Br(F) denote its Brauer group, i.e. the group of equivalence classes of finite-dimensional central simple F-algebras under tensor product, where two algebras are equivalent if they are isomorphic to matrix algebras over a common central division F-algebra. For a prime number p, let (Br F)_p denote the p-primary torsion subgroup of Br(F), consisting of all elements whose order is a power of p. A subgroup D of an abelian group A is called divisible if for every d ∈ D and every positive integer n there exists d' ∈ D with nd' = d. An abelian group is called an elementary abelian 2-group if every nonzero element has order 2.

Prove or disprove: for every field F and every prime p, at least one of the following three conditions holds:
(a) (Br F)_p = 0;
(b) (Br F)_p contains a nonzero divisible subgroup;
(c) p = 2 and (Br F)_2 is an elementary abelian 2-group.

Let $(R,\mathfrak{m})$ be a regular local ring of dimension $n$, and let $I\subseteq R$ be an ideal. Recall that the symbolic power $I^{(m)}$ is defined by
$$I^{(m)} \;=\; \bigcap_{P\in\mathrm{Ass}(R/I)} (I^m R_P\cap R),$$
and that the big height of $I$, denoted $\mathrm{bight}(I)$, is the maximum of the heights of the associated primes of $I$. Suppose $\mathrm{bight}(I)=e$, and let $r\geq 1$ be a positive integer. Determine whether each of the following containments holds:

(1) $I^{(m)} \subseteq \mathfrak{m}^{rn-r}\, I^{r}$ for all integers $m \geq rn$;

(2) $I^{(m)} \subseteq \mathfrak{m}^{rn-r-(n-1)}\, I^{r}$ for all integers $m \geq rn-(n-1)$;

(3) $I^{(m)} \subseteq \mathfrak{m}^{re-r}\, I^{r}$ for all integers $m \geq re$;

(4) $I^{(m)} \subseteq \mathfrak{m}^{re-r-(e-1)}\, I^{r}$ for all integers $m \geq re-(e-1)$.

Let $\mathcal{H}$ denote a Hardy field, i.e., a field of germs at $+\infty$ of real-valued functions defined on a half-line, closed under differentiation. A function $a\in\mathcal{H}$ is said to have polynomial growth if there exists an integer $k\ge 0$ such that $|a(t)|/t^{k}\to 0$ as $t\to\infty$. Fix $\ell\ge 1$ and let $F=\{a_1,\ldots,a_\ell\}\subset\mathcal{H}$ be a collection of Hardy-field functions of polynomial growth. Let $\operatorname{span}^{*}(F)$ denote the set of nonzero real linear combinations $a=c_1 a_1+\cdots+c_\ell a_\ell$ with $(c_1,\ldots,c_\ell)\in\mathbb{R}^\ell\setminus\{0\}$.

A family of integer sequences $\{b_1(n),\ldots,b_\ell(n)\}$ is called good for $\ell$-convergence of a single transformation if for every invertible measure-preserving system $(X,\mathcal{B},\mu,T)$ and every $f_1,\ldots,f_\ell\in L^\infty(\mu)$, the averages
$$\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N} f_1(T^{b_1(n)}x)\cdots f_\ell(T^{b_\ell(n)}x)$$
converge in $L^2(\mu)$ as $N\to\infty$.

Show that the family $\{(\lfloor a_1(n)\rfloor),\ldots,(\lfloor a_\ell(n)\rfloor)\}$ is good for $\ell$-convergence of a single transformation if and only if every $a\in\operatorname{span}^{*}(F)$ satisfies at least one of the following:
\begin{itemize}
\item $|a(t)-c\,p(t)|/\log t \to \infty$ as $t\to\infty$ for every $c\in\mathbb{R}$ and every $p\in\mathbb{Z}[t]$;
\item $a(t)-c\,p(t)\to d$ as $t\to\infty$ for some $c,d\in\mathbb{R}$ and some $p\in\mathbb{Z}[t]$;
\item $|a(t)-t/m|\le C\log t$ for all sufficiently large $t$, for some nonzero $m\in\mathbb{Z}$ and some $C>0$.
\end{itemize}

In group-based cryptography, a "platform group" is a (typically infinite, finitely presented, noncommutative) group $G$ in which cryptographic primitives—such as key-exchange protocols based on the conjugacy search problem, the simultaneous conjugacy search problem, the decomposition problem, or the word/twisted-conjugacy problems—are instantiated. For such a platform to be cryptographically suitable, it should simultaneously satisfy:

(i) elements of $G$ admit a unique, efficiently computable normal form, and the group operations (multiplication, inversion) are computable in polynomial time;
(ii) there exists an efficient method to sample elements of $G$ from a distribution with sufficiently high entropy;
(iii) the algorithmic search problems on which the proposed protocols rely (e.g. conjugacy search, membership, decomposition) are computationally hard on average for the chosen distribution, with no known subexponential attacks (including length-based attacks, linear/representation attacks, quotient attacks, or geodesic-length attacks);
(iv) the word problem in $G$ is solvable efficiently while the relevant search problems remain intractable.

Determine a finitely presented noncommutative group $G$ (or a parameterized family of such groups) that provably satisfies properties (i)–(iv) and is therefore an "appropriate" platform group for noncommutative group-based cryptography. In particular, decide whether any of the standard candidate families—braid groups $B_n$, Thompson's group $F$, polycyclic groups, free metabelian groups, small cancellation groups, Grigorchuk-type self-similar groups, or linear groups over suitable rings—provides such a platform, and justify the choice with rigorous hardness guarantees for the underlying search problems.

Background. In group-based (noncommutative) cryptography, a *platform group* is a (typically infinite, finitely presented, non-abelian) group $G$ in which cryptographic primitives—such as Diffie–Hellman-style key exchange (e.g. via commuting subgroups or conjugacy), digital signature schemes, and authentication protocols—are instantiated. The security of such schemes is generally argued by reducing the task of an adversary to the hardness of an algorithmic problem in $G$ (for example, the conjugacy search problem, the simultaneous conjugacy search problem, the decomposition problem, the membership problem, or variants of the word/geodesic length problem), often relative to a chosen probability distribution on keys.

Problem. Develop a general framework, together with verifiable criteria on a finitely presented group $G$ and on a probability distribution $\mathcal D$ on its elements, under which one can give a rigorous reduction-style proof of security (in a precisely defined adversarial model such as IND-CPA, IND-CCA, EUF-CMA, or analogous interactive authentication-soundness models) for noncommutative cryptographic primitives—public-key exchange, digital signatures, and authentication—built over $G$. In particular, identify explicit candidate platform groups $G$ and explicit hardness assumptions $\mathcal H(G,\mathcal D)$ such that:
(i) $\mathcal H(G,\mathcal D)$ can be precisely formulated as a search or decision problem with respect to $\mathcal D$;
(ii) for at least one of the primitives above, one can prove a polynomial-time reduction from breaking the primitive (in the chosen security model) to solving $\mathcal H(G,\mathcal D)$;
(iii) there is supporting evidence (worst-case to average-case reductions, generic-case complexity lower bounds, or unconditional lower bounds in restricted models) that $\mathcal H(G,\mathcal D)$ is intractable.

Equivalently: produce a noncommutative platform group together with associated cryptographic schemes that are *provably secure* in the standard sense of modern provable cryptography.

A digital signature scheme consists of a key-generation algorithm producing a public/private key pair, a signing algorithm that uses the private key to produce a signature on a message, and a verification algorithm that uses the public key to check signatures. A digital signature scheme is called noncommutative if its underlying algebraic platform (the group, ring, semiring, or algebra in which the keys, signing operation, and the hard problem reside) is noncommutative, and if the unforgeability of the scheme reduces to a computational problem (such as the conjugacy search problem, the symmetrical or polynomial symmetrical decomposition problem, a word problem, or a matrix/linear-algebraic problem) over that noncommutative platform.

Design new noncommutative digital signature schemes beyond the three previously known constructions. Concretely: exhibit explicit families of noncommutative algebraic platforms (groups, semirings, rings, or finite-dimensional associative algebras) together with signing and verification procedures such that:

(a) correctness holds: every signature produced by the signing algorithm with a valid private key is accepted by the verification algorithm with the corresponding public key;

(b) the resulting scheme is provably or heuristically existentially unforgeable under chosen-message attacks, with security reducing to a clearly stated, presumed hard computational problem on the noncommutative platform;

(c) the scheme is genuinely distinct from the three previously proposed noncommutative signature schemes, in the sense that its underlying hard problem and algebraic platform are not equivalent to theirs.

Provide such constructions, analyze their efficiency (key size, signature size, signing/verification time), and discuss the hardness assumptions required.

For an integer k ≥ 2, let C_{2k} denote the cycle on 2k vertices, and let ex(n, C_{2k}) denote the Turán number of C_{2k}, i.e. the maximum number of edges in a simple graph on n vertices that contains no subgraph isomorphic to C_{2k}. Prove or disprove: for every fixed integer k ≥ 2, there exists a positive constant c = c(k) > 0 such that
ex(n, C_{2k}) ≥ c · n^{1 + 1/k}
for all sufficiently large n.

Let $M_n$ denote the algebra of $n\times n$ complex matrices. A matrix $A\in M_k$ is said to be a compression of $B\in M_n$ (where $k\le n$) if there exists an isometry $V:\mathbb{C}^k\to\mathbb{C}^n$ (equivalently, an $n\times k$ matrix $V$ with $V^*V=I_k$) such that $A=V^*BV$. A matrix is normal if it commutes with its adjoint.

Given complex numbers $a_1,\dots,a_k$ and $b_1,\dots,b_n$ (with $k\le n$), find necessary and sufficient conditions on these two lists for the existence of normal matrices $A\in M_k$ and $B\in M_n$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item the eigenvalues of $A$, counted with multiplicity, are exactly $a_1,\dots,a_k$;
\item the eigenvalues of $B$, counted with multiplicity, are exactly $b_1,\dots,b_n$;
\item $A$ is a compression of $B$.
\end{itemize}
Equivalently, characterize precisely those pairs of multisets $\{a_i\}\subset\mathbb{C}$ and $\{b_j\}\subset\mathbb{C}$ that arise in this manner.

Setting. Consider a one-dimensional nearest-neighbor self-interacting random walk on $\mathbb{Z}$ ("excited random walk with cookies") defined as follows. To each site $x\in\mathbb{Z}$ is attached a "cookie stack" $(\omega(x,1,i))_{i\ge 1}$, where $\omega(x,\pm1,i)\in[0,1]$ with $\omega(x,1,i)+\omega(x,-1,i)=1$. When the walk visits site $x$ for the $i$-th time, it jumps to $x+1$ with probability $\omega(x,1,i)$ and to $x-1$ with probability $\omega(x,-1,i)$. A cookie at $(x,i)$ is called a "placebo" if $\omega(x,1,i)=1/2$.

Consider the following hypotheses on the random environment $\omega$:
- (SE) the cookie stacks $(\omega(x,\cdot,\cdot))_{x\in\mathbb{Z}}$ form a stationary ergodic sequence under spatial shifts;
- (IID) the cookie stacks are i.i.d.\ across sites;
- (POS1) nonnegative average drift in the $+1$ direction, i.e., $\sum_{i\ge 1}\mathbb E[\omega(0,1,i)-1/2]\ge 0$;
- (UEL) uniform ellipticity: there exists $\kappa>0$ such that $\omega(x,\pm1,i)\in[\kappa,1-\kappa]$ a.s.;
- (WEL) weak ellipticity: $\omega(x,\pm1,i)\in(0,1)$ a.s.;
- (BD) there exists $M<\infty$ such that almost surely, for every site $x$, only the first $M$ cookies are non-placebo;
- (INT) $\mathbb E\!\left[\sup\{i\in\mathbb N:\,|\omega(0,1,i)-1/2|\ge\varepsilon\}\right]<\infty$ for some $\varepsilon>0$.

Let $X_n$ be the position of the walk at time $n$ starting at $0$, and let $\delta$ denote the "criticality parameter" of the model so that the regime of interest is $\delta=1$. Under (IID), (BD), and (WEL) it is known that, at $\delta=1$ and under diffusive scaling, the rescaled process $(n^{-1/2}X_{\lfloor nt\rfloor})_{t\ge 0}$ converges in distribution to a constant multiple of the running maximum $\max_{0\le s\le t}B_s$ of a standard Brownian motion $B$.

Question. (a) Does this functional limit theorem (convergence of the rescaled walk to the running maximum of Brownian motion at $\delta=1$) continue to hold if one replaces the assumptions (IID), (BD), (WEL) by the more general assumption
$$\text{(SE)}+\text{(POS1)}+\text{(UEL)}+\text{(INT)}\,?$$
(b) If the answer to (a) is affirmative, can the nonnegative-drift assumption (POS1) also be removed?

A projective plane is an incidence structure consisting of a set of points and a set of lines, together with an incidence relation between points and lines, satisfying the following axioms:

(i) any two distinct lines are incident with exactly one common point;
(ii) any two distinct points are incident with exactly one common line;
(iii) there exist four points, no three of which are incident with a common line.

If such a plane has finitely many points, then there is a positive integer q (called the order of the plane) such that:
- the total number of points equals the total number of lines and equals q^2 + q + 1;
- every line is incident with exactly q + 1 points;
- every point is incident with exactly q + 1 lines.

For every prime power q, there exists a projective plane of order q (for example, the Desarguesian plane PG(2, F_q) over the finite field F_q).

Problem. Classify, up to isomorphism, all finite projective planes. As a (presumably easier) sub-problem, determine the exact set of positive integers q which occur as the order of some finite projective plane. In particular, decide whether every order q must be a prime power.

For a positive integer $n$, determine the smallest possible degree $d(n)$ of a real multilinear polynomial $f:\{-1,1\}^n\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f(x)\in[-1,-2/3]\cup[2/3,1]$ for every $x\in\{-1,1\}^n$;
\item $f(x)\in[2/3,1]$ whenever $\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \ge n/2$;
\item $f(x)\in[-1,-2/3]$ whenever $\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \le -n/2$.
\end{enumerate}
(There is no constraint on the values of $f$ on inputs $x$ with $-n/2<\sum_i x_i<n/2$, beyond condition (1).) Give matching upper and lower bounds on $d(n)$, in particular determining its asymptotic order as $n\to\infty$.

Let $S_n$ denote the symmetric group on $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$ and $\mathrm{Inv}(S_n)=\{\pi\in S_n : \pi^2=\mathrm{id}\}$ the set of involutions. For $\pi\in S_n$, write:
- $\mathrm{tr}(\pi)$ for the number of fixed points of $\pi$ (the trace of its permutation matrix);
- $\kappa(\pi)$ for the total number of cycles of $\pi$ (including fixed points);
- $c_j(\pi)$ for the number of cycles of $\pi$ of length exactly $j$, so that $\sum_j j\,c_j(\pi)=n$;
- $E(\pi)$ for the number of even-length cycles of $\pi$, so that $E(\pi)=0$ means every cycle of $\pi$ has odd length.

Let $S(k,j)$ be the Stirling numbers of the second kind, and let $B_m(z)$ denote the Bell polynomials defined by $\sum_{m\ge 0} B_m(z)\frac{x^m}{m!}=\exp\!\bigl(z(e^x-1)\bigr)$.

Prove or disprove the following two identities, valid for every positive integer $n$ (and every positive integer $k$ in (i), and every $z$ in (ii)):

(i) $\displaystyle \sum_{\pi\in \mathrm{Inv}(S_n)} \frac{\mathrm{tr}(\pi)^k}{n!} \;=\; \sum_{j=0}^{k} S(k,j)\,\frac{\#\mathrm{Inv}(S_{n-j})}{(n-j)!},$

(ii) $\displaystyle \sum_{\substack{\pi\in S_n\\ E(\pi)=0}} 2^{\kappa(\pi)}\, B_{\kappa(\pi)}(z) \;=\; \sum_{\pi\in S_n} (2z)^{\kappa(\pi)} \prod_{j=1}^{n} j^{\,c_j(\pi)}.$

Let $G$ be a locally compact group with left Haar measure, and let $L^1(G)$ denote its group algebra. A Segal algebra on $G$ is a dense left ideal $S^1(G)$ of $L^1(G)$ which is a Banach algebra under its own norm $\|\cdot\|_{S^1(G)}$ such that:
(i) $\|f\|_{L^1(G)} \le \|f\|_{S^1(G)}$ for all $f \in S^1(G)$;
(ii) $S^1(G)$ is left translation invariant, the map $x \mapsto L_x f$ from $G$ into $(S^1(G),\|\cdot\|_{S^1(G)})$ is continuous for each $f\in S^1(G)$, where $(L_x f)(y)=f(x^{-1}y)$;
(iii) $\|L_x f\|_{S^1(G)} = \|f\|_{S^1(G)}$ for all $x\in G$ and $f\in S^1(G)$.

A Banach algebra $A$ is called pseudo-amenable if it admits an approximate diagonal, i.e., a net $(m_\alpha)\subset A\widehat{\otimes} A$ such that $a\cdot m_\alpha - m_\alpha\cdot a \to 0$ and $\pi(m_\alpha)a \to a$ for every $a\in A$, where $\pi: A\widehat{\otimes} A\to A$ is the multiplication map.

Question: If $G$ is an amenable locally compact group and $S^1(G)$ is any Segal algebra on $G$, must $S^1(G)$ necessarily be pseudo-amenable?

Let X be a scheme of finite type over an algebraically closed field k of characteristic zero, let Γ be a finite group acting on X by scheme automorphisms, and let g be an algebra over k equipped with a Γ-action by algebra automorphisms. The equivariant map algebra M(X, g)^Γ is defined as the algebra of Γ-equivariant regular maps X → g, i.e.

   M(X, g)^Γ = { f ∈ g ⊗_k O(X) : (γ · f)(x) = γ · f(γ^{-1} · x) for all γ ∈ Γ, x ∈ X },

with pointwise algebra operations inherited from g.

The representation theory (in particular, the classification of irreducible finite-dimensional representations) of M(X, g)^Γ has been developed in the case where g is a finite-dimensional Lie algebra or finite-dimensional associative algebra. Develop the corresponding representation theory and, in particular, classify the irreducible finite-dimensional representations of M(X, g)^Γ in each of the following cases:

(a) g is a (symmetrizable) Kac–Moody Lie algebra;
(b) g is a Heisenberg Lie algebra;
(c) g is a finite-dimensional alternative algebra;
(d) g is a finite-dimensional Jordan algebra;
(e) more generally, g is an algebra of any other type for which a well-defined notion of finite-dimensional representation exists.

Let $P$ be a polygon in the plane (not assumed to be rational), and consider the billiard flow on $P$: a point particle moves in straight lines inside $P$ and reflects elastically off the sides, with reflections at vertices left undefined. Label the sides of $P$ by the symbols of a finite alphabet $\mathcal{A}$. Each billiard orbit that does not hit a vertex produces a bi-infinite word over $\mathcal{A}$ recording the sequence of sides it hits. The (full) billiard complexity $F(n)$ is the number of distinct words of length $n$ that arise as factors of such orbit codes.

For a class $\mathcal{C}$ of orbits (defined by additional restrictions), define the corresponding partial complexity $F_{\mathcal{C}}(n)$ as the number of distinct length-$n$ factors that arise from coding only orbits in $\mathcal{C}$. Examples include:
- the periodic complexity $f_P(n)$, counting length-$n$ factors that arise as factors of codes of periodic billiard orbits;
- the directional complexity $F_\theta(n)$, restricting to orbits with initial direction $\theta$;
- the positional complexity $F_z(n)$, restricting to orbits emanating from a fixed point $z \in P$.

Problem: For a general (not necessarily rational) polygon $P$, find nontrivial, explicit upper and/or lower bounds on partial billiard complexities $F_{\mathcal{C}}(n)$, in particular on the periodic complexity $f_P(n)$.

A numerical semigroup is a subset S of the nonnegative integers N that contains 0, is closed under addition, and has finite complement N\S in N. Associated to such an S are the following invariants:
- the multiplicity m(S), defined as the smallest positive element of S;
- the embedding dimension e(S), defined as the cardinality of the unique minimal system of generators of S;
- the genus g(S), defined as the cardinality of N\S (the set of gaps);
- the Frobenius number F(S), defined as the largest integer not in S (with the convention F(N)=-1);
- the type t(S), defined as the cardinality of the set of pseudo-Frobenius numbers PF(S) = { x ∈ Z\S : x + s ∈ S for every s ∈ S\{0} }.

Problem: Determine the type t(S) of a numerical semigroup S in terms of (some combination of) the invariants m(S), e(S), g(S), and F(S). More precisely, for fixed values of these invariants, describe the possible values of t(S), and in particular find sharp upper and lower bounds for t(S) given any prescribed combination of m(S), e(S), g(S), and F(S).

A numerical semigroup is a subset S of the nonnegative integers that contains 0, is closed under addition, and has finite complement in the nonnegative integers. For a numerical semigroup S, define:

- the Frobenius number F(S) as the greatest integer not belonging to S;
- the genus g(S) as the cardinality of the set of nonnegative integers not in S;
- the embedding dimension e(S) as the cardinality of the minimal system of generators of S (equivalently, the cardinality of (S \ {0}) \ ((S \ {0}) + (S \ {0}))).

Determine whether every numerical semigroup S satisfies the inequality
    e(S) · g(S) ≤ (e(S) − 1) · (F(S) + 1).

Let $V$ be a smooth complex projective variety of complex dimension $n$. For $0 \le p,q \le n$, let $h^{p,q}(V) = \dim_{\mathbb{C}} H^q(V, \Omega_V^p)$ denote the Hodge numbers of $V$, and let the Chern characteristic numbers of $V$ be the integers obtained by evaluating monomials of total degree $n$ in the Chern classes $c_1(TV),\dots,c_n(TV)$ on the fundamental class $[V]$.

Consider rational linear combinations
$$L = \sum_{p,q} a_{p,q}\, h^{p,q} + \sum_{|I|=n} b_I\, c_I,$$
with $a_{p,q}, b_I \in \mathbb{Q}$, viewed as rational-valued functions on the class of smooth complex projective varieties of dimension $n$.

Determine, in each of the following four senses of equivalence, exactly which such linear combinations $L$ are invariants of the equivalence class of $V$:
(i) orientation-preserving diffeomorphism;
(ii) (unoriented) diffeomorphism;
(iii) orientation-preserving homeomorphism;
(iv) (unoriented) homeomorphism.

That is, characterize the $\mathbb{Q}$-vector space of all $(a_{p,q}, b_I)$ such that $L(V) = L(V')$ whenever $V$ and $V'$ are smooth complex projective varieties related by the chosen equivalence, and identify those that are not invariant.

A Finsler metric on a smooth manifold $M$ is a continuous function $F:TM\to[0,\infty)$ that is smooth and positive on $TM\setminus\{0\}$, positively homogeneous of degree one in the fiber variable (i.e. $F(x,\lambda v)=\lambda F(x,v)$ for all $\lambda>0$), and whose vertical Hessian $g_{ij}(x,v)=\tfrac12\partial^2 F^2/\partial v^i\partial v^j$ is positive definite on $TM\setminus\{0\}$. The flag curvature $K(x,v,P)$ generalizes the sectional curvature of Riemannian geometry; $F$ is said to have constant flag curvature $K\in\mathbb{R}$ if $K(x,v,P)\equiv K$ for all admissible flags $(x,v,P)$. A Finsler metric is called Zoll if every (oriented) geodesic of $F$ is closed. A Killing vector field of $F$ is a vector field on $M$ whose (local) flow consists of isometries of $F$.

Construct an explicit example of a Finsler metric $F$ on the 2-sphere $S^2$ such that:
(i) $F$ has constant flag curvature;
(ii) $F$ is not Zoll, i.e. at least one geodesic of $F$ is not closed;
(iii) $F$ admits no nontrivial Killing vector field.

Alternatively, prove that no such Finsler metric exists.

Let $(M,g)$ be a smooth closed (compact, without boundary) connected pseudo-Riemannian manifold. Suppose that $L: TM \to TM$ is a smooth $(1,1)$-tensor field on $M$ satisfying all of the following conditions:

(i) $L \not\equiv 0$;
(ii) $L$ is self-adjoint with respect to $g$, i.e. $g(LX,Y) = g(X,LY)$ for every pair of vector fields $X,Y$ on $M$;
(iii) $L$ is parallel with respect to the Levi-Civita connection $\nabla$ of $g$, i.e. $\nabla L = 0$;
(iv) $L$ is nilpotent, i.e. there exists an integer $k \ge 1$ such that $L^{k} = 0$ as an endomorphism of $TM$.

Determine whether such a triple $(M,g,L)$ can exist. More precisely, decide whether there is any closed pseudo-Riemannian manifold $(M,g)$ admitting a non-zero, $g$-self-adjoint, $\nabla$-parallel, nilpotent $(1,1)$-tensor field, and if so, classify the possible $(M,g,L)$ up to local isometry.

For a class X of groups, define the following class operators:
- H(X) = the class of all homomorphic images of groups in X;
- S(X) = the class of all groups isomorphic to a subgroup of some group in X;
- P(X) = the class of all groups isomorphic to an unrestricted direct product ∏_{i∈I} G_i of an arbitrary family (G_i)_{i∈I} of groups in X;
- P_f(X) = the class of all groups isomorphic to a direct product of a finite family of groups in X.

(By Birkhoff's HSP theorem, H(S(P(X))) is the variety of groups generated by X.)

Question: Is it true that for every class M consisting of metabelian groups (i.e., groups whose commutator subgroup is abelian), one has the inclusion
   H(S(P_f(M))) ⊆ S(H(P(S(M))))?

A group $G$ is said to be self-similar if there exists a subgroup $H \le G$ of finite index together with a faithful action of $G$ on the regular rooted tree $T_m$ of valence $m = [G:H]$ that is induced by a simple virtual endomorphism (equivalently, $G$ admits a faithful self-similar representation as automorphisms of some regular rooted tree). For positive integers $d$ and $c$, let $\mathcal{T}_{d,c}$ denote the class of finitely generated torsion-free nilpotent groups that can be generated by $d$ elements and are of nilpotency class exactly $c$.

It is known that:
(i) every group in $\mathcal{T}_{d,2}$ is self-similar, for every $d \ge 1$;
(ii) every group in $\mathcal{T}_{2,3}$ is self-similar;
(iii) there exist groups in $\mathcal{T}_{4,3}$ that are not self-similar.

Question: Does there exist a group in $\mathcal{T}_{3,3}$ (i.e., a $3$-generated torsion-free nilpotent group of nilpotency class $3$) that is not self-similar?

Let $G$ be a group. For a positive integer $n$, let $S_n$ denote the symmetric group on $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$, and define the normalized Hamming distance on $S_n$ by
$$d(\sigma,\tau) = \frac{1}{n}\,\#\{i\in\{1,\dots,n\} : \sigma(i)\neq\tau(i)\}.$$
Call $G$ sofic if for every finite subset $F\subseteq G$ with $1\in F$ and every $\varepsilon>0$ there exist a positive integer $n$ and a map $\varphi : F \to S_n$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\varphi(1)$ is the identity permutation;
\item $d(\varphi(gh),\varphi(g)\varphi(h)) < \varepsilon$ for all $g,h\in F$ with $gh\in F$;
\item for every $g\in F\setminus\{1\}$, the permutation $\varphi(g)$ has no fixed points (equivalently, $d(\varphi(g),\mathrm{id})=1$).
\end{enumerate}
Question: Is every group sofic?

Consider the following vectorial modified nonlinear Schrödinger model for the slowly varying envelope of a short optical pulse. Let $v=(v_1,v_2):\mathbb{R}_\tau\times\mathbb{R}^2_{x_\perp}\times\mathbb{R}_z\to\mathbb{C}^2$ be the unknown. Fix a small parameter $\varepsilon>0$, real constants $\alpha_1,\alpha_2$, and a real vector $\alpha_3\in\mathbb{R}^3$. Write $\nabla=(\nabla_\perp,\partial_z)$ for the spatial gradient, $\Delta_\perp=\partial_{x_1}^2+\partial_{x_2}^2$ for the transverse Laplacian, and $|v|^2=|v_1|^2+|v_2|^2$, $v\cdot v=v_1^2+v_2^2$. Let $P_2(\varepsilon\nabla)$ denote a fixed second-order linear differential operator (a polynomial of degree $2$ in $\varepsilon\nabla$ with constant coefficients arising from the dispersion relation of the underlying Maxwell system). Consider the Cauchy problem
$$ iP_2(\varepsilon\nabla)\partial_\tau v + (\Delta_\perp+\alpha_1\partial_z^2)v + i\alpha_2 v + \tfrac{1}{3}\bigl(1+i\varepsilon\,\alpha_3\cdot\nabla\bigr)\bigl((v\cdot v)\,\overline{v} + 2|v|^2 v\bigr)=0,$$
with prescribed initial data $v|_{\tau=0}=v_0$.

Establish, for this two-component complex vector equation, the analogues of the standard questions formulated for the corresponding scalar modified NLS:
(i) global existence versus finite-time blow-up of solutions for suitable classes of initial data;
(ii) the occurrence and description of pulse splitting phenomena;
(iii) the role and rigorous treatment of the self-steepening term $i\varepsilon\,\alpha_3\cdot\nabla$;
(iv) the interaction of mixed dispersive, dissipative ($\alpha_2$) and nonlinear effects;
(v) the validity and time scale of approximation of solutions of the full underlying Maxwell equations by solutions of this vectorial envelope equation on long time intervals as $\varepsilon\to 0$.

Consider a polling system consisting of $N\ge 2$ queues $Q_1, \dots, Q_N$ served by a single server. Customers arrive at queue $Q_i$ according to a stochastic arrival process (e.g. Poisson with rate $\lambda_i$), require i.i.d.\ service times with finite mean, and the server incurs a switch-over time $S_{ij}$ when moving from $Q_i$ to $Q_j$. Fix the loads $\rho_i=\lambda_i\mathbb{E}[B_i]$ with total load $\rho=\sum_i\rho_i<1$. Scale the switch-over times by a factor $s>0$ (so each $S_{ij}$ is replaced by $sS_{ij}$) and let $s\to\infty$. Under the cyclic exhaustive (or cyclic gated) discipline with a fixed pre-determined visit order, it is known that, after appropriate scaling, the cycle times converge to a deterministic limit and the scaled waiting-time distribution at each queue converges to a uniform distribution on an explicit interval determined by $\rho_i$ and the mean switch-overs.

Provide a complete classification of the triples (service discipline, visit discipline, scheduling discipline) for which this same deterministic limiting behaviour holds as the switch-over times tend to infinity. The classification should cover:
(i) service disciplines beyond exhaustive and gated (e.g.\ $k$-limited, time-limited, globally gated, Bernoulli, mixed gated/exhaustive, etc.);
(ii) visit disciplines including dynamic visit orders, where the next queue to be served is not fixed a priori but is determined adaptively (e.g.\ based on queue states);
(iii) scheduling disciplines governing the order of service within a visit.

Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on (i)–(iii) under which, as the scaling parameter of the switch-over times tends to infinity, the scaled cycle time converges to a deterministic constant and the scaled waiting-time distributions at the queues converge to the corresponding uniform limiting distributions.

A quandle is a set $Q$ equipped with a binary operation $*: Q\times Q\to Q$ such that (i) $a*a=a$ for all $a\in Q$, (ii) for each $b\in Q$ the map $x\mapsto x*b$ is a bijection of $Q$, and (iii) $(a*b)*c=(a*c)*(b*c)$ for all $a,b,c\in Q$. Classical (rack/quandle) homology is defined via the chain complex $C_n^R(Q)=\mathbb Z[Q^n]$ with boundary $\partial(x_1,\dots,x_n)=\sum_{i=2}^n(-1)^i\big[(x_1,\dots,\hat x_i,\dots,x_n)-(x_1*x_i,\dots,x_{i-1}*x_i,\hat x_i,\dots,x_n)\big]$, and quandle homology $H_n^Q(Q;A)$ is obtained by quotienting out degenerate subcomplexes; the associated cocycle state-sum invariants distinguish classical knots and links.

A virtual quandle is a quandle $Q$ equipped with an additional bijection $f:Q\to Q$ that is a quandle automorphism (so $f(a*b)=f(a)*f(b)$), used to color the virtual crossings of a virtual knot or link diagram while the quandle operation colors classical crossings; virtual quandle colorings are invariant under the generalized Reidemeister moves of virtual knot theory.

Problem. Construct a homology (and dual cohomology) theory $H_*^{VQ}(Q,f;A)$ for virtual quandles $(Q,f)$ that:
(a) reduces to ordinary (rack/quandle) homology when $f=\mathrm{id}_Q$;
(b) admits a state-sum cocycle invariant assigning, to each virtual knot or link diagram $D$ together with a virtual-quandle coloring, a Boltzmann weight using $H^n_{VQ}(Q,f;A)$ that is invariant under all generalized Reidemeister moves (including the virtual moves and the mixed move);
(c) determine which classes of virtual knots and links are distinguished by the resulting invariants but cannot be distinguished by classical quandle cocycle invariants applied to the underlying combinatorial data, and exhibit explicit pairs of virtual knots separated only by these new invariants.

A virtual knot diagram is a 4-valent plane graph whose vertices are decorated either as classical crossings (with over/under information) or as virtual crossings (depicted by a small circle), considered up to generalized Reidemeister moves. To each virtual diagram D one associates an atom, namely a closed (possibly nonorientable) surface F together with an embedded 4-valent graph G ⊂ F (the underlying graph of D) whose complementary regions are 2-colored (black/white) so that opposite local regions at every vertex receive the same color; the atom genus g(D) is the genus of F. Equivalently, g(D) measures the smallest genus of a thickened surface in which the diagram can be realized as an alternating-like projection compatible with its Gauss data. For a diagram D with n classical crossings let ⟨D⟩ denote the Kauffman bracket and span⟨D⟩ its breadth; one always has span⟨D⟩ ≤ 4n − 4g(D).

Address the following:

(a) Develop effective methods that, for a given concrete virtual knot diagram D, certify that the atom genus g(D) is minimal among all virtual diagrams representing the same virtual knot.

(b) Develop effective methods that certify minimality of the classical crossing number of D, including the more general setting in which one projects the underlying knot to a closed orientable surface Σ_h of genus h ≥ 0 and seeks the minimum number of crossings of any such projection representing the given (virtual) knot type.

(c) In particular, derive lower bounds for these crossing numbers (in the plane and on higher-genus surfaces) from:
   (i) the Kauffman bracket polynomial and its span;
   (ii) Thistlethwaite-type spanning-tree expansions of the Kauffman bracket;
   (iii) atom-based invariants;
   (iv) Khovanov homology of virtual links (for example via the height/width of its bigraded support).

Determine to what extent each of these invariants yields sharp estimates, and identify classes of diagrams for which the resulting bounds prove minimality of crossing number and/or atom genus.

A topological group $G$ is called a pro-Lie group if it is isomorphic (as a topological group) to a closed subgroup of a (possibly infinite) Cartesian product of finite-dimensional real Lie groups; equivalently, $G$ is complete and every identity neighborhood of $G$ contains a closed normal subgroup $N$ such that $G/N$ is a finite-dimensional Lie group. Recall that a topological group is Polish if its underlying topological space is separable and completely metrizable, separable if it has a countable dense subset, and compactly generated if it is generated algebraically by some compact subset.

Develop a satisfactory structure and Lie theory for the following three classes of pro-Lie groups, with particular emphasis on the connected case:

(a) Polish pro-Lie groups;
(b) separable pro-Lie groups;
(c) compactly generated pro-Lie groups.

Specifically, characterize when a pro-Lie group belongs to each of these classes in terms of its Lie algebra $\mathfrak{L}(G)$ and its quotients by suitable closed normal subgroups, establish appropriate analogues for these classes of the structure theorems known for connected pro-Lie groups (such as the Levi–Mal'cev decomposition, the existence of maximal compact subgroups, and the exponential function $\exp:\mathfrak{L}(G)\to G$), and determine to what extent classical results valid for connected locally compact groups (e.g., open mapping theorems, closed-subgroup theorems, the structure of abelian and solvable groups) extend to each of (a), (b), and (c).

Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^4$ be a bounded domain with smooth boundary $\partial\Omega$, and let $S$ denote the best Sobolev constant for the embedding $H^1_0(\Omega)\hookrightarrow L^4(\Omega)$, i.e.
$$S=\inf_{u\in H^1_0(\Omega)\setminus\{0\}}\frac{\int_\Omega|\nabla u|^2\,dx}{\left(\int_\Omega u^4\,dx\right)^{1/2}}.$$
Fix $2<q<4$ and positive parameters $a,b,\lambda,\delta>0$. Consider the Kirchhoff-type Brezis–Nirenberg problem
$$-\Bigl(a+b\int_\Omega|\nabla u|^2\,dx\Bigr)\Delta u=\lambda|u|^{q-2}u+\delta|u|^{2}u\quad\text{in }\Omega,\qquad u=0\text{ on }\partial\Omega.\tag{$\ast$}$$

A known existence theorem asserts: if $bS^2<\delta<2bS^2$, $\Omega$ is strictly star-shaped, and one of the following holds,
\begin{itemize}
\item[(C1)] $a>0$ and $\lambda>0$ is sufficiently small;
\item[(C2)] $\lambda>0$ and $a>0$ is sufficiently large;
\item[(C3)] $a>0$, $\lambda>0$, and $\delta/b>S^2$ is sufficiently close to $S^2$;
\end{itemize}
then $(\ast)$ admits a nontrivial weak solution.

Question: Are the hypotheses $\delta<2bS^2$, the strict star-shapedness of $\Omega$, and the auxiliary parameter conditions (C1)–(C3) all necessary in order to guarantee the existence of a nontrivial solution to $(\ast)$? Equivalently, can existence of a nontrivial solution still be ensured if any of these hypotheses is dropped or weakened?

Let T be a one-dimensional primitive substitution tiling of the real line whose inflation (substitution) has scaling factor λ > 1, and assume:

(i) λ is a Pisot number, i.e., a real algebraic integer greater than 1 all of whose other Galois conjugates lie strictly inside the open unit disk;
(ii) λ is unimodular, i.e., the constant term of its minimal polynomial over ℤ is ±1;
(iii) the inflation is recognizable, so that the associated tiling space Ω_T (the orbit closure under translation of T, equipped with the local-matching/tiling metric) is a compact metric space on which ℝ acts by translation.

Let Ȟ¹(Ω_T; ℚ) denote the first Čech cohomology group of Ω_T with rational coefficients, and let d = deg(λ) be the algebraic degree of λ over ℚ. The translation ℝ-action on Ω_T preserves a unique invariant Borel probability measure μ; the action is said to have pure point (pure discrete) dynamical spectrum if the associated unitary representation on L²(Ω_T, μ) is generated by eigenfunctions.

Question: If rank_ℚ Ȟ¹(Ω_T; ℚ) = d, must the translation action on (Ω_T, μ) have pure point spectrum?

Let $n\ge 2$. Let $\mathcal{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ denote the real Hardy space, and for a locally integrable function $b$ let $[b,T]f := bTf - T(bf)$ denote the commutator of multiplication by $b$ with an operator $T$. Write $I$ for the Riesz potential of order $1$ (so $\widehat{If}(\xi)=|\xi|^{-1}\widehat{f}(\xi)$ up to a constant), and $R_\alpha$, $\alpha=1,\dots,n$, for the Riesz transforms on $\mathbb{R}^n$.

Recall that an $\mathcal{H}^1$-atom is a function $a\in L^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n)$ supported in some ball $B$ with $\|a\|_{L^\infty}\le |B|^{-1}$ and $\int a=0$. Given $b\in L^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n)$, a $b$-atom is a standard $\mathcal{H}^1$-atom $a$ that satisfies in addition $\int a\,b\,dx=0$.

Fix a bounded Lipschitz map $H:\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\to\mathbb{R}$, fix $u\in W^{1,n}(\mathbb{R}^n,\mathbb{R}^{n+1})$, and put $b := H\circ u\in W^{1,n}(\mathbb{R}^n)\cap L^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n)$. The Jacobian $n$-form is $du^1\wedge\cdots\wedge du^n$, identified with the scalar $\det(Du)$ (or, more generally, with the collection of $n\times n$ minors of $Du$ when $u$ has $n+1$ components), which is known to lie in $\mathcal{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$.

Determine:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] the largest subspace $V\subset \mathcal{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ such that $[b,I]:V\to L^{n/(n-1)}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is bounded; the largest such $V$ if $L^{n/(n-1)}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is replaced by $W^{1,1}(\mathbb{R}^n)$; and whether this $V$ contains all $n\times n$ minors of $Du$;
\item[(b)] the largest subspace $V\subset \mathcal{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ such that $[b,R_\alpha]:V\to L^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is bounded for every $\alpha=1,\dots,n$, and whether this $V$ contains all $n\times n$ minors of $Du$;
\item[(c)] whether $du^1\wedge\cdots\wedge du^n$ admits an atomic decomposition into $b$-atoms;
\item[(d)] whether the answers to (a)–(c) change under the additional assumption that $u$ solves an $H$-system, i.e.\ $\Delta u = 2\,H(u)\,u_{x_1}\wedge\cdots\wedge u_{x_n}$ in the sense of distributions.
\end{enumerate}

A multiple hypergeometric sum is a finite or infinite sum of the form
$$S(n) \;=\; \sum_{k_1,\dots,k_r} F(n,k_1,\dots,k_r),$$
where the summand $F$ is a proper hypergeometric term, i.e. a ratio of products of factorials, Pochhammer symbols, and exponential factors in the integer variables $n,k_1,\dots,k_r$. Classical single-sum identities such as those of Gauss, Saalschütz, Dixon, and Watson evaluate or reduce $\sum_k F(n,k)$ in closed form under explicit balance/parameter conditions, and the Wilf–Zeilberger / Gosser–Zeilberger algorithms provide a systematic method for proving and discovering such single-sum identities.

Develop an analogous systematic theory and algorithmic toolkit for multiple hypergeometric sums with $r \geq 2$ summation indices that:

(1) recognizes when $S(n)$ admits a reduction to a sum of strictly lower nesting depth (fewer summation indices) or to a closed-form hypergeometric expression;

(2) automatically conjectures such a reduction from the parameter data of $F$;

(3) certifies the resulting identity by an algorithmic proof procedure;

and which produces a finite catalogue of named "master" reduction identities playing, for multi-sums, the role that the Gauss, Saalschütz, Dixon, and Watson identities play for single sums. Concretely, applications should include the simplification of sums arising as connection coefficients between families of orthogonal polynomials and as expansions of multiple iterated integrals.

Question: Does such a systematic theory and algorithmic toolkit exist, and if so, give it explicitly; otherwise, prove obstructions to its existence.

Let X be a (real or complex) infinite-dimensional separable Banach space, and let B_X denote its closed unit ball, equipped with the weak topology inherited from X. Let K(B_X) denote the collection of all weakly compact subsets of B_X, partially ordered by set inclusion.

Recall that two directed partially ordered sets (P,≤_P) and (Q,≤_Q) are Tukey equivalent if there exist maps f: P → Q and g: Q → P such that f sends cofinal subsets of P to cofinal subsets of Q and g sends cofinal subsets of Q to cofinal subsets of P; equivalently, there exists a directed partial order into which both embed cofinally.

Consider the following four "standard" directed posets:
(i) the trivial one-element poset {0};
(ii) N^N, the set of functions from the natural numbers to themselves, ordered by everywhere domination f ≤ g ⇔ f(n) ≤ g(n) for all n;
(iii) K(Q), the family of compact subsets of the rationals Q (with the usual topology), ordered by inclusion;
(iv) [c]^{<ω}, the family of finite subsets of the continuum c, ordered by inclusion.

Question: Is it relatively consistent with ZFC that there exists a separable Banach space X such that the poset K(B_X) is not Tukey equivalent to any of {0}, N^N, K(Q), or [c]^{<ω}?

Let $(\Omega,\Sigma,\mu)$ denote the Lebesgue measure space on $[0,1]$, and let $X$ be a real Banach space with topological dual $X^{*}$. A function $f:[0,1]\to X$ is called strongly measurable if it is the $\mu$-almost everywhere limit of a sequence of simple $X$-valued functions. For a strongly measurable function $f:[0,1]\to X$, set $\|f\|_{\infty}=\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{t\in[0,1]}\|f(t)\|_{X}$, and let $L^{\infty}([0,1],X)$ be the Banach space of (equivalence classes of) strongly measurable functions with $\|f\|_{\infty}<\infty$.

A sequence $(f_n)$ of strongly measurable functions $f_n:[0,1]\to X$ is said to:
\begin{itemize}
\item converge \emph{scalarly in measure} to $0$ if, for every $x^{*}\in X^{*}$, the scalar sequence $(\langle x^{*},f_n(\cdot)\rangle)$ converges to $0$ in measure on $[0,1]$;
\item converge \emph{scalarly almost everywhere} to $0$ if, for every $x^{*}\in X^{*}$, the scalar sequence $(\langle x^{*},f_n(t)\rangle)$ converges to $0$ for $\mu$-almost every $t\in[0,1]$.
\end{itemize}

The space $X$ is said to have property $(D_{\infty})$ if every sequence $(f_n)$ of strongly measurable functions $f_n:[0,1]\to X$ that is bounded in $L^{\infty}([0,1],X)$ and converges scalarly in measure to $0$ admits a subsequence $(f_{n_k})$ converging scalarly almost everywhere to $0$.

Question: Does $X=L^{1}[0,1]$ have property $(D_{\infty})$?

Let Σ = (G, σ) be a signed graph, where G = (V, E) is a finite simple graph and σ : E → {+1, −1} is a sign function on edges. The sign of a circle (i.e., a cycle subgraph) C in Σ is defined as the product ∏_{e ∈ E(C)} σ(e); the circle C is called positive if its sign is +1 and negative otherwise. The signed graph Σ is balanced if every circle of Σ is positive, and unbalanced otherwise. A Hamiltonian circle of Σ is a circle that uses every vertex of G exactly once.

Suppose Σ is unbalanced and possesses at least one Hamiltonian circle. Determine whether the following must necessarily hold:
(a) Σ contains a negative Hamiltonian circle;
(b) Σ contains a positive Hamiltonian circle;
(c) Σ contains both a positive and a negative Hamiltonian circle.

Equivalently, characterize those unbalanced signed graphs admitting a Hamiltonian circle that fail to contain a positive Hamiltonian circle, those that fail to contain a negative one, and those that contain both types.

Consider the two-dimensional diffraction problem for a time-harmonic scalar wave passing through two finite slits in an otherwise impenetrable planar screen. Specifically, let the screen be the set
S = (R \ (I_1 ∪ I_2)) × {0} ⊂ R^2,
where I_1 = (a_1, b_1) and I_2 = (a_2, b_2) are two disjoint bounded open intervals on the x-axis (the two slits). Let k > 0 be a fixed wavenumber, and let u^{inc}(x, y) be a prescribed incident plane wave satisfying the Helmholtz equation
Δu^{inc} + k^2 u^{inc} = 0   in R^2.
Seek the total field u(x, y) = u^{inc}(x, y) + u^{sc}(x, y), where the scattered field u^{sc} satisfies:
1. (Helmholtz equation) Δu^{sc} + k^2 u^{sc} = 0 in R^2 \ S;
2. (Boundary condition on the screen) u = 0 on S (Dirichlet/sound-soft case), or alternatively ∂u/∂n = 0 on S (Neumann/sound-hard case);
3. (Sommerfeld radiation condition at infinity) lim_{r→∞} r^{1/2}(∂_r u^{sc} − i k u^{sc}) = 0 uniformly in the angular variable, where r = (x^2 + y^2)^{1/2};
4. (Edge condition) appropriate Meixner-type finiteness of energy near each of the four endpoints a_1, b_1, a_2, b_2.

Problem: Provide a rigorous and explicit closed-form mathematical solution u^{sc} of this two-slit diffraction problem; that is, exhibit u^{sc} in explicit form (for instance via convergent series, special-function representations, or evaluable integral transforms) and prove that the resulting field satisfies all four conditions above. The same question is posed for the corresponding quantum-mechanical analogue, in which u is the stationary wavefunction satisfying the same Helmholtz equation with absorbing slit boundaries describing Young's double-slit setup.

Consider the following open-shop scheduling problem. We are given m machines $M_1, \dots, M_m$ and n jobs. Each job $j \in \{1,\dots,n\}$ consists of m operations $O_{1j}, \dots, O_{mj}$, where operation $O_{ij}$ must be processed on machine $M_i$ for exactly one unit of time (i.e., $p_{ij} = 1$). Each job $j$ has a release date $r_j \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$ (no operation of job $j$ may start before time $r_j$), a due date $d_j \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$, and a nonnegative weight $w_j$. A schedule assigns to every operation $O_{ij}$ an integer start time $s_{ij} \geq r_j$ such that:
(i) on each machine, no two operations overlap in time;
(ii) for each job, no two of its operations overlap in time;
(iii) the order in which the m operations of a job are processed may be chosen freely (open-shop).
Job $j$ is on time if $\max_i (s_{ij}+1) \leq d_j$, and tardy ($U_j = 1$) otherwise. The objective is to minimize $\sum_j w_j U_j$, the weighted number of tardy jobs.

Question: Is this problem (denoted $O \mid p_{ij}=1, r_j \mid \sum w_j U_j$) fixed-parameter tractable when parameterized by the number m of machines? That is, does there exist a computable function $f$ and a constant $c$ such that the problem can be solved in time $f(m) \cdot |I|^c$, where $|I|$ is the input size?

In general relativity, a black hole spacetime is modeled by a 4-dimensional Lorentzian manifold $(M,g)$ satisfying the Einstein field equations. While in the stationary case the boundary of a black hole is unambiguously captured by notions such as the event horizon, the Killing horizon, the apparent horizon, the trapping horizon, and the isolated horizon (which all coincide), in genuinely time-dependent situations these notions diverge and none is universally accepted as the correct mathematical model of the evolving boundary of a black hole.

Recall that on a spacelike 2-surface $S \subset M$ with outward unit spacelike normal $r^a$ and future unit timelike normal $\tau^a$ (within a chosen 3-slice), the outgoing and ingoing null normals are $\ell^a = \tau^a + r^a$ and $n^a = \tau^a - r^a$, with associated expansions $\Theta_{(\ell)}$ and $\Theta_{(n)}$. A 2-surface is marginally (outer) trapped if $\Theta_{(\ell)} = 0$ and $\Theta_{(n)} < 0$. One existing proposal defines a dynamical horizon as a smooth 3-dimensional spacelike submanifold $H \subset M$ that admits a foliation by closed marginally trapped 2-surfaces, but this notion is non-unique (foliation-dependent), can fail to be spacelike (becoming null or timelike) in dynamical regimes, and is not known to satisfy desirable quasi-local laws (e.g.\ a generalized second law) in full generality.

Problem. Formulate a mathematically precise, geometrically natural, and quasi-local definition of a "dynamical horizon" — that is, a hypersurface (or family of 2-surfaces) in a general 4-dimensional Lorentzian spacetime $(M,g)$ — such that:
(i) the definition reduces to the event/Killing/isolated-horizon notion in the stationary limit;
(ii) the resulting horizon is uniquely determined by local geometric data (independent of an arbitrary choice of foliation or slicing of spacetime);
(iii) it remains well-defined under generic time-dependent evolution, allowing matter/radiation flux across it;
(iv) it satisfies analogues of the laws of black-hole mechanics, including a monotonicity (area-increase / second-law) property under physically reasonable energy conditions; and
(v) it correctly captures the intuitive notion of the evolving boundary of a black hole in general dynamical settings.

Consider a quantum theory of gravity in which a black hole forms from the gravitational collapse of matter initially prepared in a pure quantum state, and subsequently evaporates completely via Hawking radiation. Semiclassical analysis (using quantum field theory on a fixed curved background) predicts that the emitted Hawking radiation is in a thermal mixed state determined essentially by the black hole's mass, charge, and angular momentum, so that the final state after complete evaporation appears to be mixed. This contradicts unitary evolution in quantum mechanics, which requires a pure initial state to evolve to a pure final state and forbids loss of quantum information.

The problem is to determine, within a consistent theory that combines general relativity with quantum mechanics, whether the quantum information encoding the initial pure state of the collapsing matter is preserved throughout the formation and complete evaporation of the black hole, and to give a precise mechanism describing how this information is (or is not) recovered in the final state of the radiation. A complete resolution should specify:

(i) whether the global evolution of the formation-and-evaporation process is unitary;
(ii) how, if unitarity holds, the fine-grained entropy of the Hawking radiation evolves with time (in particular, whether it follows the Page curve, rising and then falling to zero as the black hole evaporates);
(iii) the physical mechanism by which information initially behind the horizon is transferred to, or encoded in, the outgoing radiation, in a way consistent with locality and the equivalence principle (or a precise account of which of these principles must be modified, and how).

Provide such a resolution, or prove that no resolution compatible with the standard postulates of quantum mechanics exists.

Let $G$ be a finitely generated group, and let $\mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_+(S^1)$ denote the group of orientation-preserving $C^{\infty}$ diffeomorphisms of the circle, equipped with the $C^{\infty}$ topology. A homomorphism $\varphi: G \to \mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_+(S^1)$ is called a faithful action if it is injective. A nonempty closed $\varphi(G)$-invariant subset $K \subseteq S^1$ is called minimal if every $\varphi(G)$-orbit of a point in $K$ is dense in $K$; the action is called minimal if $K = S^1$ is minimal, and $K$ is called an exceptional minimal set if it is minimal and homeomorphic to a Cantor set.

Suppose $\varphi_0: G \to \mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_+(S^1)$ is a faithful action that admits an exceptional minimal set. Does there exist a continuous path $t \mapsto \varphi_t$, $t \in [0,1]$, in the space of faithful homomorphisms $G \to \mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_+(S^1)$ (with respect to the $C^{\infty}$ topology), starting at the given $\varphi_0$, such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item for every $t \in [0,1)$, the action $\varphi_t$ admits an exceptional minimal set; and
\item the action $\varphi_1$ is minimal (i.e., every $\varphi_1(G)$-orbit is dense in $S^1$)?
\end{itemize}

Let G = (V,E) be a finite simple graph. A matching of G is a set M ⊆ E of pairwise non-adjacent edges; the induced subgraph ⟨M⟩ is the subgraph of G whose vertex set V(M) consists of all endpoints of edges in M and whose edge set is the set of all edges of G with both endpoints in V(M).

Given a graph property P (a class of graphs closed under isomorphism), call a matching M of G a P-matching if ⟨M⟩ has property P. A P-matching M is called maximal if M is not properly contained in any larger P-matching of G. Define the parameters
  β_P(G) = max{ |M| : M is a P-matching of G },
  β_P^-(G) = min{ |M| : M is a maximal P-matching of G }.

Examples of properties P that have been studied in this framework include:
 (i) P = "the induced subgraph is 1-regular" (the standard induced/strong matching);
 (ii) P = "acyclic" (matchings whose induced subgraph is a forest, i.e. uniquely restricted matchings);
 (iii) P = "connected";
 (iv) P = "disconnected";
 (v) P = "the induced subgraph has maximum degree at most k" for fixed k ≥ 1;
 and analogous variants obtained from other natural graph properties.

Problem. For each of the above choices of P, establish nontrivial upper bounds for β_P(G) and nontrivial lower bounds for β_P^-(G), expressed in terms of standard graph invariants of G (such as the number of vertices n, number of edges m, maximum degree Δ, minimum degree δ, or matching number).

Let G = (V, E) be a finite simple graph. A matching M ⊆ E is a set of pairwise non-adjacent edges. For a matching M, denote by ⟨M⟩ the subgraph of G induced by the set of endpoints of the edges in M. Given a graph property P (a class of graphs closed under isomorphism), call M a P-matching if ⟨M⟩ has property P. A P-matching M is maximal if no P-matching of G properly contains M, and maximum if |M| is as large as possible among all P-matchings of G.

Standard instances of P include:
 (i) P = "M is an induced matching of G" (the only edges of ⟨M⟩ are those of M);
 (ii) P = "⟨M⟩ has M as its unique perfect matching" (uniquely restricted matching);
 (iii) P = "⟨M⟩ is acyclic" (acyclic matching);
 (iv) P = "⟨M⟩ is connected" (connected matching);
 (v) P = "⟨M⟩ is disconnected" (disconnected matching).

For each such property P, develop a structural characterization theorem that determines, given a graph G and a P-matching M of G, exactly when M is a maximum P-matching of G (as opposed to merely a maximal P-matching). The characterization should be intrinsic to the pair (G, M) — for example, in terms of augmenting-type structures in G relative to M analogous to Berge's theorem for ordinary matchings — and not merely the tautological condition |M| = ν_P(G).

Let $F=\mathbb{F}_q$ be a finite field of order $q=p^n$ with $p$ prime, and let $\psi\colon F\to\mathbb{C}^\times$ be the canonical additive character $\psi(x)=\exp(2\pi i\,\mathrm{Tr}_{F/\mathbb{F}_p}(x)/p)$. For an integer $d$ with $\gcd(d,q-1)=1$ (an "invertible exponent" over $F$), define the Weil sum of the binomial $x^d+ax$ by
$$ W_{F,d}(a)\;=\;\sum_{x\in F}\psi(x^d+ax),\qquad a\in F. $$
The Weil spectrum of $(F,d)$ is the set
$$ \mathcal{W}_{F,d}\;=\;\{\,W_{F,d}(a)\;:\;a\in F\,\}\subseteq\mathbb{C}. $$
We say $\mathcal{W}_{F,d}$ is three-valued if $|\mathcal{W}_{F,d}|=3$.

Define an equivalence relation on pairs $(F,d)$ as follows: $(F,d)\sim(F',d')$ if and only if $F=F'=\mathbb{F}_{p^n}$ and there exists an integer $j$ with $0\le j<n$ such that $d'\equiv p^j d \pmod{q-1}$, or $d\, d'\equiv p^j\pmod{q-1}$ (i.e., $d$ and $d'$ are related by multiplication by a power of $p$ modulo $q-1$, possibly composed with multiplicative inversion modulo $q-1$). Pairs equivalent under this relation produce identical Weil spectra.

The known three-valued cases consist of the following infinite families (each up to the above equivalence):
1. $d\equiv 1\pmod{q-1}$ trivial cases excluded; the nontrivial known families include the Gold exponents $d=p^k+1$ with $n/\gcd(n,k)$ odd over $\mathbb{F}_{p^n}$;
2. the Kasami exponents $d=p^{2k}-p^k+1$ with appropriate parameters;
3. the Welch exponent $d=2^{(n-1)/2}+3$ over $\mathbb{F}_{2^n}$, $n$ odd;
4. the Niho exponents over $\mathbb{F}_{2^n}$, $n$ odd;
5. and the remaining sporadic and infinite families catalogued in the standard tables of three-valued Weil spectra.

Question: Is every pair $(F,d)$, where $F$ is a finite field and $d$ is an invertible exponent over $F$ such that the Weil spectrum $\mathcal{W}_{F,d}$ is three-valued, equivalent (under the equivalence relation above) to a pair belonging to one of the known families listed above? Equivalently, is the known classification of three-valued Weil spectra of binomials over finite fields complete?

Let $d_1, d_2$ be positive integers and let $A$ be a positive semidefinite matrix acting on a $d_1 d_2$-dimensional complex Hilbert space, written as a $d_2 \times d_2$ array of blocks $A_{jk}$, $1 \le j,k \le d_2$, each of size $d_1 \times d_1$. Let $M = \sum_{j=1}^{d_2} A_{jj}$ denote the sum of its diagonal blocks (which is a $d_1 \times d_1$ positive semidefinite matrix). 

Determine whether one can always find $d_2$ positive semidefinite block matrices $B_1, B_2, \dots, B_{d_2}$ of the same block structure (each is $d_1 d_2 \times d_1 d_2$, partitioned into $d_2 \times d_2$ blocks of size $d_1 \times d_1$), such that:
1. $\operatorname{rank}(B_m) \le d_1$ for every $m = 1, \dots, d_2$;
2. $\sum_{j=1}^{d_2} (B_m)_{jj} = M$ for every $m = 1, \dots, d_2$;
3. $A = \dfrac{1}{d_2} \sum_{m=1}^{d_2} B_m$.

Background. The Deift–Zhou nonlinear steepest-descent method provides asymptotic analysis of oscillatory matrix Riemann–Hilbert problems: given an oriented contour Σ ⊂ ℂ and a jump matrix V(z; t) depending on a large parameter, one seeks a sectionally analytic function Y(z) with Y(z)→I as z→∞ satisfying Y_+(z) = Y_-(z) V(z; t) on Σ, and one extracts large-parameter asymptotics by deforming contours and constructing local and global parametrices. For 2×2 problems (e.g., orthogonal polynomials, one-matrix models, KdV) and for certain 3×3 problems (e.g., associated with two specific tractable settings), this analysis has been carried out in detail.

Problem. Develop a general nonlinear steepest-descent / Riemann–Hilbert asymptotic method that applies to generic n×n matrix Riemann–Hilbert problems for arbitrary n ≥ 3, in a form sufficient to analyze:

(a) the biorthogonal / multi-orthogonal polynomial Riemann–Hilbert problems that arise in multi-matrix random matrix models (e.g., chains of coupled Hermitian matrices with potentials V_1,…,V_p and coupling constants), and Hermitian matrix models with an external source A (probability measure proportional to exp(−n tr(V(M) − AM)) dM);

(b) systems of multiple orthogonal polynomials with respect to several weights w_1,…,w_r on ℝ (both type I and type II), including the determination of strong large-degree asymptotics of these polynomials and of the associated correlation kernels;

(c) Hermite–Padé (simultaneous Padé) approximation problems for tuples of analytic functions, with uniform error estimates;

(d) consequent arithmetic applications, such as irrationality and linear-independence-over-ℚ statements for values of classical constants and special functions.

Specifically: produce, for arbitrary n, (i) a g-function / equilibrium-measure framework on a multi-sheeted Riemann surface that yields the appropriate global parametrix, (ii) a construction of local parametrices at branch points and other critical points generalizing the Airy and Painlevé parametrices known in the 2×2 and 3×3 cases, and (iii) a small-norm Riemann–Hilbert argument that establishes the resulting asymptotic expansion uniformly in the relevant parameters.

Let $N \geq 2$ and $1 \leq W \leq N-1$ be integers. A symmetric random band matrix of dimension $N$ with bandwidth $W$ is an $N\times N$ real symmetric random matrix $M = (M_{ij})$ such that the entries $\{M_{ij} : i \le j\}$ are independent, $M_{ij} = 0$ whenever $|i-j| > W$, and the nonzero entries are centered with a common variance scaling (e.g.\ $\mathbb{E}|M_{ij}|^2 = 1/W$ for $|i-j|\le W$).

It is expected that, after appropriate rescaling, the bulk local eigenvalue statistics of $M$ exhibit a sharp transition as $W$ varies with $N\to\infty$:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $W$ is small, the local eigenvalue statistics in the bulk converge to a Poisson point process;
\item if $W$ is large, the local eigenvalue statistics in the bulk converge to the Gaudin–Mehta (GOE sine-kernel) process.
\end{itemize}

Problem. Prove that there is a sharp transition in the bulk local eigenvalue statistics of symmetric random band matrices, and determine the threshold. Specifically, show that the transition occurs in a narrow window around $W \asymp \sqrt{N}$: namely, the bulk local statistics are Poisson when $W \ll \sqrt{N}$ and Gaudin–Mehta when $W \gg \sqrt{N}$, as $N \to \infty$.

Let $(X,g)$ be a compact Riemannian manifold (possibly with boundary) whose geodesic flow is ergodic / chaotic, and let $\{\psi_k\}_{k\ge 1}$ be an orthonormal basis of real-valued eigenfunctions of the (Dirichlet) Laplace–Beltrami operator with eigenvalues $0\le \lambda_1^2\le \lambda_2^2 \le \cdots \to \infty$, so that $-\Delta\psi_k=\lambda_k^2\psi_k$. For each $k$ and a base point $x_0\in X$, consider the rescaled eigenfunction at the wavelength scale,
$$\Psi_{k,x_0}(y) := \psi_k\!\left(\exp_{x_0}(y/\lambda_k)\right), \qquad y\in T_{x_0}X,$$
viewed on balls of radius $R$ (microscopic scale, $R$ fixed) or radius $R_k\to\infty$ with $R_k=o(\lambda_k)$ (intermediate / mesoscopic scale).

Let $\Phi$ denote the isotropic monochromatic Gaussian random wave on $\mathbb{R}^d$ ($d=\dim X$): the centered Gaussian field with covariance equal to the spherical average of $e^{i\langle\xi,\,\cdot\,\rangle}$ over unit vectors $\xi\in S^{d-1}$ (Berry's random wave model). For manifolds with boundary, replace $\Phi$ by the appropriate boundary-adapted Gaussian ensemble.

Question. Prove or disprove: for a "generic" chaotic $(X,g)$, along a density-one subsequence of eigenfunctions, and for almost every base point $x_0\in X$, the rescaled fields $\Psi_{k,x_0}$ converge in distribution (in $C^\infty_{\mathrm{loc}}$, or as random tempered distributions) to $\Phi$, both at microscopic scale and at every intermediate scale $1\ll R_k\ll \lambda_k$. Equivalently, establish rigorously that the statistics of high-energy chaotic eigenfunctions agree with those of the Gaussian random-wave ensemble for the following observables:
(i) the pointwise value distribution of $\psi_k$;
(ii) two-point and higher correlation functions at separations of order $\lambda_k^{-1}$;
(iii) the geometry and topology of the nodal set $\{x:\psi_k(x)=0\}$ (e.g. Hausdorff measure, Euler characteristic);
(iv) the number, volumes, and topology of nodal domains (connected components of $\{\psi_k\ne 0\}$).

Let $A$ and $B$ be semisimple unital complex Banach algebras, and let $T : A \to B$ be a linear map that is:
- unital, i.e. $T(1_A) = 1_B$;
- surjective;
- invertibility-preserving, i.e. for every $x \in A$, if $x$ is invertible in $A$ then $T(x)$ is invertible in $B$.

For elements $x, y$ in a unital algebra, define the Jordan product $x \circ y = \tfrac{1}{2}(xy + yx)$. A Jordan epimorphism is a surjective linear map $\Phi$ between unital algebras satisfying $\Phi(x \circ y) = \Phi(x) \circ \Phi(y)$ for all $x, y$.

Question: Must every such map $T$ be a Jordan epimorphism?

Let $X$ be a compact complex space endowed with a Hermitian metric form $\omega$, and let $\Delta^k \subset \mathbb{C}^k$ denote the open unit polydisk. For a meromorphic mapping $f : \Delta^k \to X$, let $\|Df\|$ denote the (pointwise) norm of its differential measured with respect to $\omega$ and the standard Euclidean metric on $\Delta^k$, let $\mathrm{Vol}(f(\Delta^k))$ denote the volume of the image (counted without multiplicity) computed with respect to $\omega$, and let $\Gamma_f \subset \Delta^k \times X$ denote the closure of the graph of $f$ over the set where $f$ is holomorphic, with $\mathrm{Vol}(\Gamma_f)$ computed with respect to the product Hermitian metric.

Fix constants $R > 0$ and $C_1 > 0$, and consider the class $\mathcal{J}_R$ of all meromorphic mappings $f : \Delta^k \to X$ satisfying
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\|Df(z)\| > R$ for all $z$ in the domain of holomorphy of $f$;
\item[(b)] $\mathrm{Vol}(f(\Delta^k)) \le C_1$.
\end{enumerate}

Prove that there exists a constant $C_2 = C_2(X, R, C_1)$ such that
$$\mathrm{Vol}(\Gamma_f) \le C_2 \qquad \text{for every } f \in \mathcal{J}_R.$$

Let $S^{m-1}=\{x=(x_1,\dots,x_m)\in\mathbb{R}^m:\ x_i\ge 0,\ \sum_{i=1}^m x_i=1\}$ denote the $(m-1)$-dimensional simplex. A quadratic stochastic operator (QSO) on $S^{m-1}$ is a map $V:S^{m-1}\to S^{m-1}$ defined by
$$ (Vx)_k = \sum_{i,j=1}^m P_{ij,k}\, x_i x_j,\qquad k=1,\dots,m, $$
where the coefficients satisfy $P_{ij,k}=P_{ji,k}\ge 0$ and $\sum_{k=1}^m P_{ij,k}=1$ for all $i,j$. The trajectory of a point $x^{(0)}\in S^{m-1}$ under $V$ is the sequence $x^{(n+1)}=V(x^{(n)})$, $n\ge 0$.

Given two QSOs $V_1$ and $V_2$ on the same simplex $S^{m-1}$, define the one-parameter family of QSOs
$$ V_\lambda = \lambda V_1 + (1-\lambda) V_2,\qquad \lambda\in[0,1]. $$

Establish a general theory connecting the dynamical properties of $V_\lambda$ with those of $V_1$ and $V_2$. Specifically, determine for arbitrary $V_1$ and $V_2$:
1. how the set of fixed points of $V_\lambda$ depends on $\lambda$ and on the fixed-point sets of $V_1$ and $V_2$;
2. which invariant subsets, attractors, and $\omega$-limit sets of $V_1$ and $V_2$ persist for $V_\lambda$ at all $\lambda\in(0,1)$, which disappear, and which new ones emerge;
3. how regular versus non-ergodic (irregular) behaviour, periodic trajectories, and convergence properties of trajectories of $V_\lambda$ are inherited from, or fail to be inherited from, the corresponding properties of $V_1$ and $V_2$;
4. for which classes of QSOs (e.g., Volterra, bistochastic, dissipative, non-Volterra) the class is preserved under convex combinations, and for which it is not.

Consider a massive galaxy cluster modeled as a hot, X-ray emitting plasma halo in approximate hydrostatic equilibrium, in which the radiative cooling rate per unit volume scales as n^2 Λ(T), with n the gas number density, T the temperature, and Λ(T) the cooling function (so that the cooling rate depends steeply on density). At the cluster centre sits a supermassive black hole that, when active in a "radio mode", episodically (intermittently) injects mechanical/thermal energy into the surrounding intracluster medium via jets and inflated bubbles, with a duty cycle strictly less than 1 (so that not every cluster, and not every epoch in a given cluster, is observed to host central radio activity).

Question: Determine whether such intermittent, centrally concentrated radio-mode energy injection can, on average, deposit energy in a spatial and temporal pattern that quasi-stably suppresses the development of a classical cooling flow throughout the halo — i.e. balances radiative losses at all radii where the cooling time is shorter than the Hubble time — given:
(i) the strongly super-linear (∝ n^2) density dependence of radiative cooling, which makes simultaneous global stabilization at all radii difficult;
(ii) the observation that a non-trivial fraction of clusters exhibit no detectable central radio activity at a given time;
(iii) the requirement that any successful heating prescription must reproduce observed cool-core temperature and density profiles without overheating cluster cores or evacuating gas from the centre.

Provide either a feedback prescription (specifying jet power as a function of accretion or cooling rate, duty cycle, and spatial energy deposition profile) for which one can prove stabilization in this sense, or an argument that no such intermittent, centrally driven prescription can achieve it.

Background. In Bayesian inference, given a hypothesis class $\mathcal{H}$ one assigns a prior probability distribution over $\mathcal{H}$. A guiding "symmetry principle" says that, absent further information, one should treat hypotheses symmetrically. For a finite class $\mathcal{H} = \{h_1,\dots,h_n\}$ this is realized by the uniform prior $P(h_i)=1/n$. When $\mathcal{H}$ is parameterized by $\theta$ in a smooth manifold $\Theta$, a natural strengthening is reparametrization invariance: if $\phi=g(\theta)$ is a smooth bijective change of coordinates, then the prior densities $p_\Theta$ and $p_\Phi$ should be related by the usual Jacobian rule, so that the induced measure on $\mathcal{H}$ is independent of the chosen parameterization. For a regular parametric family with Fisher information matrix $I(\theta)$, the Jeffreys prior $p(\theta)\propto \sqrt{\det I(\theta)}$ is reparametrization invariant; on a compact parameter space it can typically be normalized to a proper probability distribution. On non-compact parameter spaces such as $\Theta=\mathbb{N}$ (or any countably infinite discrete set) or $\Theta=\mathbb{R}$ (or $\mathbb{R}^d$), the Jeffreys prior and other symmetry-derived rules frequently yield improper (non-normalizable) distributions, which are often unsuitable as genuine priors (for example, they need not give well-defined predictive distributions or admit a coherent decision-theoretic interpretation).

Problem. Formulate and justify a general principle for assigning a prior probability distribution to an arbitrary (possibly non-compact, infinite, discrete or continuous) hypothesis class $\mathcal{H}$ such that:
(1) it reduces to the uniform prior when $\mathcal{H}$ is finite;
(2) it is invariant under a suitably specified group of admissible reparametrizations / symmetries of $\mathcal{H}$;
(3) it always yields a proper (normalizable) probability distribution on $\mathcal{H}$, in particular on $\mathcal{H}$ parameterized by $\mathbb{N}$ or by $\mathbb{R}^d$;
(4) it agrees, in the regular compact case, with Jeffreys' prior (or with another well-motivated invariant prior).

Either exhibit such a construction together with the precise symmetry group it respects and a proof of properness and invariance, or prove an impossibility result showing that no construction can simultaneously satisfy (1)–(4) under a stated minimal set of axioms.

Solomonoff's universal a priori semimeasure on the set of finite binary strings is defined by M(x) = sum over programs p such that U(p) starts with x of 2^{-|p|}, where U is a fixed universal monotone Turing machine. Solomonoff prediction assigns to a finite observed sequence x the conditional predictive probability M(xy)/M(x) for continuations y. Two classical alternative inductive frameworks are: (i) Carnap's confirmation theory, in which a confirmation function c(h,e) measuring the degree to which evidence e confirms hypothesis h is built from a logical-probability measure on a first-order or propositional language, typically via continuum-of-inductive-methods parameters; and (ii) Laplace's rule of succession, which after observing s successes in n Bernoulli trials predicts probability (s+1)/(n+2) for the next success.

Provide a principled extension or clarification of Solomonoff-style universal induction that simultaneously:

1. Yields, as identifiable special cases or quantitatively comparable limits, both Carnap's continuum of inductive methods (for an appropriate restriction to monadic predicate languages and exchangeable sequences) and Laplace's rule of succession;

2. Applies to continuous model classes, i.e. defines a universal predictor over hypothesis classes consisting of probability measures on R^N (or more generally on a Polish space) parametrized by continuous parameters, with computable convergence and dominance properties analogous to the discrete case (where M dominates every lower-semicomputable semimeasure up to a multiplicative constant);

3. Incorporates prior domain knowledge K in a principled way, by replacing the universal reference machine U with a knowledge-conditioned reference and proving that the resulting predictor (a) reduces to standard Solomonoff induction when K is empty, (b) is invariant under equivalent encodings of K up to bounded additive loss in the relevant log-loss bounds, and (c) admits explicit predictive convergence theorems generalizing Solomonoff's theorem that the expected total squared error in predicting a computable distribution is bounded by a constant depending only on the program length of that distribution.

Construct such an extension and prove the corresponding reduction, dominance, and convergence theorems, or demonstrate an obstruction showing no such unified framework can exist.

Setting. Consider the framework of universal algorithmic agents acting in computable environments. Let an environment μ be a computable probability measure over interaction histories of an agent receiving percepts (observations and rewards r_t ∈ [0,1]) and emitting actions, and let π denote a policy (agent). Define the expected discounted future value V^π_μ of policy π in environment μ in the standard universal-AI manner. The (incomputable) intelligence order relation (IOR) declares π ⪰ π' iff V^π_μ ≥ V^{π'}_μ for every computable environment μ in some reference class, weighted (or quantified) by a universal prior such as the Solomonoff prior 2^{−K(μ)}.

Problem. Construct a computable, resource-bounded surrogate ⪰_c of the intelligence order relation (or of a meaningful restricted special case of it) such that:

(a) ⪰_c is decidable, or at least effectively approximable, given explicit time and space resource bounds on both the policies being compared and on the environments in the reference class;

(b) ⪰_c induces a practically implementable intelligence test for agents, in analogy with how the C-Test for compression-based intelligence was obtained from the incomputable Kt-complexity-based definition of universal sequence prediction;

(c) ⪰_c is justified by an explicit choice of resource-bounded complexity measure (for example Levin's Kt complexity, Schmidhuber's Speed Prior, time-bounded Kolmogorov complexity K^t, distinguishing complexity, or another variant from algorithmic information theory).

Determine which notion of resource-bounded complexity, or which alternative universal-style prior, is most appropriate for defining ⪰_c, and prove that the resulting relation (i) refines or approximates the original IOR in a precise sense (e.g. agreeing with it in the limit of unbounded resources), (ii) is computable within stated resource bounds, and (iii) yields a feasible benchmark protocol that can rank concrete agents.

Consider a physical system whose dynamics is governed by laws that may include stochastic or quantum-mechanical indeterminism. Suppose that, in measurement-like situations, definite "facts" (e.g. the click of a detector) come into being in an apparently irreversible manner, while the underlying microscopic equations of motion (Schrödinger evolution, or a time-symmetric stochastic process) are invariant under time reversal.

(a) Formulate a precise sense in which a stochastic or indeterministic dynamical law on a state space can be said to "define a direction of time," distinguishing it from cases in which the law is statistically time-symmetric. In particular, determine whether the mere presence of stochasticity in a Markovian or quantum-stochastic evolution suffices to single out a preferred temporal orientation, or whether an additional asymmetric input (such as a low-entropy initial condition or an asymmetric boundary condition) is required.

(b) Consider the following candidate accounts of the irreversible occurrence of measurement outcomes:
  (i) a fundamental collapse law added to the unitary dynamics (as in objective collapse theories or von Neumann's projection postulate);
  (ii) the view that such events lie outside the dynamical laws altogether;
  (iii) the view that the apparent irreversibility is merely an increase of an observer's information (Copenhagen-style);
  (iv) determination by hidden variables not represented in the conventional ensemble (as in pilot-wave theory);
  (v) indeterministic branching of observers into many worlds (Everett-type interpretations).

For each candidate, decide whether it is capable of accounting for the temporal asymmetry of measurement outcomes without tacitly invoking the same asymmetry it is intended to explain (for instance, by presupposing a time-asymmetric initial state, a preferred branching direction, or an asymmetric updating rule for information or hidden variables).

Consider a closed quantum cosmological system whose global state is a vector $|\Psi(t)\rangle$ in a Hilbert space $\mathcal{H} = \mathcal{H}_S \otimes \mathcal{H}_E$ (system tensor environment), evolving by a unitary group $U(t) = e^{-iHt/\hbar}$ generated by a time-reversal-symmetric Hamiltonian $H$. Decoherence is the suppression of off-diagonal elements of the reduced density matrix $\rho_S(t) = \mathrm{Tr}_E |\Psi(t)\rangle\langle \Psi(t)|$ in some quasi-classical pointer basis $\{|q\rangle\}$, i.e. $\langle q|\rho_S(t)|q'\rangle \to 0$ for $q\neq q'$ as $t$ increases.

Two candidate accounts of measurement are admitted:
(a) a non-unitary collapse law replacing $U(t)$ at random times by a projection, yielding a time-asymmetric dynamics;
(b) purely unitary Everettian branching, in which the unitarily evolved $|\Psi(t)\rangle$ decomposes into approximately orthogonal, dynamically autonomous components ("branches"/quasi-classical worlds).

The problem is the following. Identify, in mathematically explicit form, a condition $\mathcal{C}$ on the initial global wave function $|\Psi(t_0)\rangle$ (an "initial-state hypothesis") such that, under unitary evolution by $H$:
1. For all $t > t_0$ the reduced state $\rho_S(t)$ exhibits monotone (or near-monotone) decoherence in a preferred quasi-classical basis, while recoherence — i.e. the spontaneous return of off-diagonal coherences — is generically suppressed for cosmologically relevant timescales;
2. The unitary evolution of $|\Psi(t)\rangle$ admits a stable branching decomposition $|\Psi(t)\rangle = \sum_\alpha c_\alpha(t) |\psi_\alpha(t)\rangle$ into quasi-classical worlds carrying a definite forward temporal orientation (records, retarded correlations);
3. In the alternative scenario (a), the same condition $\mathcal{C}$ on $|\Psi(t_0)\rangle$ accounts for the observed time-asymmetry of decoherence (retarded rather than advanced entanglement), in tandem with the time-asymmetric collapse law.

Determine such a condition $\mathcal{C}$, prove that it implies (1)–(3), and decide whether (b) alone (no collapse) suffices, given $\mathcal{C}$, to recover a uniquely oriented arrow of time within the resulting Everettian branch structure.

Let $k$ be a field and let $n>2$ be an integer. Consider the polynomial ring $k[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ and its group of polynomial automorphisms $\mathrm{GA}_n(k)$. Inside $\mathrm{GA}_n(k)$, let:
- $\mathrm{Aff}_n(k)$ denote the affine subgroup, consisting of all automorphisms of the form $x_i \mapsto \sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j + b_i$ with $(a_{ij})\in \mathrm{GL}_n(k)$ and $b_i\in k$;
- $\mathrm{BA}_n(k)$ denote the triangular (de Jonquières) subgroup, consisting of automorphisms of the form $x_i \mapsto \alpha_i x_i + f_i(x_{i+1},\dots,x_n)$ with $\alpha_i\in k^\times$ and $f_i\in k[x_{i+1},\dots,x_n]$;
- $\mathrm{TA}_n(k) := \langle \mathrm{Aff}_n(k),\,\mathrm{BA}_n(k)\rangle$ denote the tame subgroup.

Determine whether $\mathrm{Aff}_n(k)$ is a maximal subgroup of $\mathrm{TA}_n(k)$; that is, whether there exists no subgroup $H$ with $\mathrm{Aff}_n(k) \subsetneq H \subsetneq \mathrm{TA}_n(k)$.

Consider low-mass Population II red giants (metal-poor stars with mass ≲ 1 M☉, typified by stars in Galactic globular clusters) evolving along the Red Giant Branch (RGB) and the Asymptotic Giant Branch (AGB). During these phases such stars lose a non-negligible fraction of their envelope through stellar winds, and the integrated mass loss controls subsequent evolution (horizontal-branch morphology, post-AGB tracks, white-dwarf masses, surface chemistry).

In current stellar evolution computations the mass-loss rate Ṁ is typically incorporated through empirical prescriptions (e.g. a Reimers-type law Ṁ = −η L R / M with a tunable efficiency parameter η, or the Bloecker/Vassiliadis–Wood prescriptions for the AGB) that were calibrated against bright Population I giants and then extrapolated to the Population II regime.

Provide a description of mass loss along the RGB and AGB of Population II giants that satisfies all of the following:

(a) it is physically grounded, i.e. derived from an identified driving mechanism (radiation pressure on dust and molecules, acoustic/Alfvén wave or magnetically driven winds, pulsation-driven outflows, chromospheric activity, binarity, or a specified combination thereof) rather than a purely empirical fitting formula;

(b) it specifies the characteristic mass-loss timescale τ_ML = M/Ṁ as a function of stellar parameters along each evolutionary phase;

(c) it gives the explicit functional dependence of the mass-loss efficiency on the global stellar parameters (luminosity L, effective temperature T_eff, radius R, current mass M, surface gravity g) and on the metallicity Z (or [Fe/H]);

(d) it is calibrated against, and consistent with, empirical determinations of total RGB and AGB mass loss obtained directly for Population II systems (for example from horizontal-branch and white-dwarf masses in globular clusters of varying metallicity, and from circumstellar dust emission of metal-poor giants);

(e) it can be implemented in stellar evolution codes without requiring extrapolation of Population I calibrations into the Population II regime.

The problem is to construct and validate such a mass-loss law.

In the theory of stellar evolution, a Thermally Pulsing Asymptotic Giant Branch (TPAGB) star undergoes recurrent helium-shell flashes followed by a "third dredge-up" (3DU): an episode in which the convective envelope penetrates inward into regions previously processed by hydrogen and helium burning, mixing freshly synthesized elements (notably carbon and s-process elements) into the surface layers. The location and efficiency of this mixing crucially shape the surface chemical composition of TPAGB stars but, in current one-dimensional stellar evolutionary codes, the boundary between the convective envelope and the underlying radiative intershell region must traverse a layer with steep discontinuities in chemical composition and in radiative opacity. Standard local mixing-length-type prescriptions, together with simple convective-boundary criteria (Schwarzschild or Ledoux), do not by themselves determine a unique inward extent for the dredge-up, and as a consequence existing fully evolutionary AGB calculations parametrize the efficiency of the 3DU by means of one or more tunable free parameters (such as an overshooting length, a diffusive-mixing scale, or an imposed dredge-up parameter λ).

Problem. Formulate, within the framework of a one-dimensional spherically symmetric stellar evolution model coupled to a treatment of time-dependent turbulent convection, a closed mathematical prescription for convective mixing across a stable interface separating two fluids with prescribed jumps in mean molecular weight μ and in Rosseland mean opacity κ, such that:

(a) the prescription is derived from, or rigorously consistent with, the underlying hydrodynamic equations governing convective motions in the presence of composition and opacity gradients (e.g., the Reynolds-averaged Navier–Stokes equations with chemical species transport);

(b) it provides a uniquely determined inward extent and time evolution of the convective–radiative boundary during a thermal pulse cycle, without recourse to an arbitrary free parameter quantifying the efficiency of the third dredge-up;

(c) when implemented in a TPAGB stellar evolution computation, it reproduces the dredge-up depth and the resulting surface abundance evolution as a deterministic function of the stellar mass, metallicity, and pulse number.

Either exhibit such a prescription and prove that conditions (a)–(c) are satisfied, or prove that within the class of one-dimensional time-dependent convection models no such prescription exists.

Let $M$ be a smooth compact Riemannian manifold with boundary $\Sigma = \partial M$, and let $E, F \to M$ be Hermitian vector bundles over $M$. Let $A : C^\infty(M, E) \to C^\infty(M, F)$ be a general linear first-order elliptic differential operator. In a collar neighborhood $[0, \varepsilon) \times \Sigma$ of the boundary, write $A$ in the form
$$A = J(x)\bigl(\partial_x + B(x)\bigr) + \text{(lower-order terms)},$$
where $J(x)$ is a bundle isomorphism $E|_{\{x\}\times\Sigma} \to F|_{\{x\}\times\Sigma}$ and $B(x)$ is a first-order elliptic operator on $\Sigma$ (no assumption is made that $A$ is of Dirac type, nor that $J$ and $B$ are independent of $x$ or unitary/self-adjoint).

Determine to what extent the following structural results, classically established for Dirac-type operators with product structure near the boundary, persist for such general $A$:

(i) the existence of a canonical Calderón projection onto the Cauchy data space of $A$, given as a pseudodifferential projection with a transparent symbolic description;

(ii) the Lagrangian property of the Cauchy data space of $A$ with respect to a suitable symplectic structure on the boundary sections;

(iii) the existence of self-adjoint Fredholm extensions of $A$ realized by regular pseudodifferential boundary conditions;

(iv) a Cobordism Theorem expressing the vanishing of an index/signature invariant when $A$ extends across the boundary;

(v) continuous dependence of the Calderón projection, Cauchy data space, self-adjoint Fredholm extensions, and their spectral data under continuous variation of the operator $A$, the underlying domain $M$, and the boundary geometry.

Identify precisely which of (i)–(v) hold for arbitrary first-order elliptic $A$, which require additional hypotheses, and what minimal hypotheses on $A$ (weaker than Dirac-type with product structure) suffice.

Consider an A-body quantum system (a nucleus) governed by a rotationally invariant Hamiltonian H acting on the antisymmetric Fock space, and consider the variational ground-state problem obtained by minimizing the energy E[D] = Tr(HD) over many-body density operators D that are themselves rotationally scalar (i.e. invariant under the action of SO(3)). For such a scalar D, the associated one-body density ρ(r) depends only on the radial coordinate r = |r| and reduces to a monopole radial profile ρ0(r) : [0, ∞) → [0, ∞) with ∫ ρ0(r) 4π r^2 dr = A. By a Hohenberg–Kohn-type argument restricted to this scalar subspace, the energy functional reduces to a scalar functional F0[ρ0] of the radial profile alone.

Formulate, in terms of the radial monopole profile ρ0(r) only, mathematically precise criteria — for instance involving (a) the magnitude of the surface slope ρ0'(rs) at a suitably defined surface radius rs (e.g. where ρ0(rs) equals half its central value), (b) the diffuseness/skin width of ρ0, (c) the radial moments ⟨r^k⟩ = ∫ r^k ρ0(r) 4π r^2 dr / A and combinations thereof, or (d) integral functionals of ρ0 — that distinguish:

(i) spherical nuclei from axially deformed nuclei;
(ii) "hard" nuclei (rigid against shape fluctuations) from "soft" nuclei;
(iii) triaxially deformed nuclei from axially symmetric ones;
(iv) halo nuclei (with anomalously extended low-density tails) from ordinary nuclei.

Specifically: identify quantitative functionals Φ[ρ0] whose values, computed on the scalar minimizer of F0, serve as signatures of each of the above structural categories, and prove rigorous implications (or sharp inequalities/thresholds) linking the value of Φ[ρ0] to the underlying intrinsic shape characteristics.

For a positive integer n, let $ inom{n}{k}$ denote the usual binomial coefficient and define
$$a_n :=  inom{2n}{n}\sum_{k=0}^{n} inom{n}{k} inom{n+2k}{2k} inom{2k}{k}(-324)^{n-k}.$$
For an odd prime p, let $\left(	frac{\cdot}{p}ight)$ denote the Legendre symbol.

Prove or disprove the following statements.

(1) For every prime $p>5$,
$$\sum_{n=0}^{p-1} rac{357n+103}{2160^{n}}\,a_n \;\equiv\; p\left(	frac{-1}{p}ight)\!\left(54+49\left(	frac{-1}{p}ight)ight) \pmod{p^{2}}.$$

(2) For every prime $p>5$, with $a_n$ as above,
$$\sum_{n=0}^{p-1} rac{a_n}{2160^{n} inom{2n}{n}^{-1}\cdot inom{2n}{n}}\;=\;\sum_{n=0}^{p-1} rac{1}{2160^{n}}\sum_{k=0}^{n} inom{n}{k} inom{n+2k}{2k} inom{2k}{k}(-324)^{n-k} inom{2n}{n}$$
satisfies, modulo $p^{2}$:
• $4x^{2}-2p$ if $\left(	frac{-1}{p}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{3}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{5}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{7}ight)=1$ and $p=x^{2}+105y^{2}$;
• $2x^{2}-2p$ if $\left(	frac{-1}{p}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{7}ight)=1,\ \left(	frac{p}{3}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{5}ight)=-1$ and $2p=x^{2}+105y^{2}$;
• $2p-12x^{2}$ if $\left(	frac{-1}{p}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{3}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{5}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{7}ight)=-1$ and $p=3x^{2}+35y^{2}$;
• $2p-6x^{2}$ if $\left(	frac{-1}{p}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{7}ight)=-1,\ \left(	frac{p}{3}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{5}ight)=1$ and $2p=3x^{2}+35y^{2}$;
• $20x^{2}-2p$ if $\left(	frac{-1}{p}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{5}ight)=1,\ \left(	frac{p}{3}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{7}ight)=-1$ and $p=5x^{2}+21y^{2}$;
• $10x^{2}-2p$ if $\left(	frac{-1}{p}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{3}ight)=1,\ \left(	frac{p}{5}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{7}ight)=-1$ and $2p=5x^{2}+21y^{2}$;
• $28x^{2}-2p$ if $\left(	frac{-1}{p}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{5}ight)=-1,\ \left(	frac{p}{3}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{7}ight)=1$ and $p=7x^{2}+15y^{2}$;
• $14x^{2}-2p$ if $\left(	frac{-1}{p}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{3}ight)=-1,\ \left(	frac{p}{5}ight)=\left(	frac{p}{7}ight)=1$ and $2p=7x^{2}+15y^{2}$;
• $0$ if $\left(	frac{-105}{p}ight)=-1$.

(3) Show that the following Ramanujan-type identity holds:
$$\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} rac{357n+103}{2160^{n}} inom{2n}{n}\sum_{k=0}^{n} inom{n}{k} inom{n+2k}{2k} inom{2k}{k}(-324)^{n-k}= rac{90}{\pi}.$$

For integers $b$ and $c$, define the generalized central trinomial coefficient $T_k(b,c)$ as the coefficient of $x^k$ in the polynomial $(x^2+bx+c)^k$; equivalently,
$$T_k(b,c)=\sum_{j=0}^{\lfloor k/2\rfloor}\binom{k}{2j}\binom{2j}{j}c^{j}b^{k-2j}.$$
For an odd prime $p$ and integer $a$ coprime to $p$, let $\left(\frac{a}{p}\right)$ denote the Legendre symbol, and let $\delta_{p,n}=1$ if $p=n$ and $0$ otherwise.

Prove or disprove the following two claims.

(I) For every odd prime $p>5$,
$$\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\left(\frac{T_k(38,212)}{(-16)^k}\right)^{\!3}\equiv\left(\frac{-5}{p}\right)\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\left(\frac{T_k(38,212)}{20^k}\right)^{\!3}\pmod{p^2},$$
and this common value modulo $p^2$ equals
- $4x^2-2p$ if $\left(\frac{2}{p}\right)=\left(\frac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\frac{p}{5}\right)=1$ and $p=x^2+30y^2$;
- $12x^2-2p$ if $\left(\frac{p}{3}\right)=1$, $\left(\frac{2}{p}\right)=\left(\frac{p}{5}\right)=-1$ and $p=3x^2+10y^2$;
- $2p-8x^2$ if $\left(\frac{2}{p}\right)=1$, $\left(\frac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\frac{p}{5}\right)=-1$ and $p=2x^2+15y^2$;
- $20x^2-2p$ if $\left(\frac{p}{5}\right)=1$, $\left(\frac{2}{p}\right)=\left(\frac{p}{3}\right)=-1$ and $p=5x^2+6y^2$;
- $0$ if $\left(\frac{-30}{p}\right)=-1$ (i.e.\ the sum is $\equiv 0\pmod{p^2}$, with the customary correction $p\,\delta_{p,7}$).

In addition,
$$\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}(28k+15)\,\frac{T_k(38,212)^3}{(-16)^{3k}}\equiv p\!\left(124-19\left(\frac{-1}{p}\right)\right)\pmod{p^2}.$$

(II) For every odd prime $p\neq 7$,
$$\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\left(\frac{T_k(110,572)}{32^k}\right)^{\!3}\equiv\left(\frac{-14}{p}\right)\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\left(\frac{T_k(110,572)}{(-28)^k}\right)^{\!3}\pmod{p^2},$$
and this common value modulo $p^2$ equals
- $4x^2-2p$ if $\left(\frac{-2}{p}\right)=\left(\frac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\frac{p}{7}\right)=1$ and $p=x^2+42y^2$;
- $8x^2-2p$ if $\left(\frac{p}{7}\right)=1$, $\left(\frac{-2}{p}\right)=\left(\frac{p}{3}\right)=-1$ and $p=2x^2+21y^2$;
- $12x^2-2p$ if $\left(\frac{-2}{p}\right)=1$, $\left(\frac{p}{3}\right)=\left(\frac{p}{7}\right)=-1$ and $p=3x^2+14y^2$;
- $24x^2-2p$ if $\left(\frac{p}{3}\right)=1$, $\left(\frac{-2}{p}\right)=\left(\frac{p}{7}\right)=-1$ and $p=6x^2+7y^2$;
- $p\,\delta_{p,19}$ if $\left(\frac{-42}{p}\right)=-1$.

In addition,
$$\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}(684k+329)\,\frac{T_k(110,572)^3}{2^{15k}}\equiv p\!\left(\left(\frac{-2}{p}\right)+1091\right)\pmod{p^2}.$$

Here $x,y\in\mathbb{Z}$ in each representation $p=ax^2+by^2$.

Let T_k(b,c) denote the generalized (third-kind) Chebyshev-type polynomials defined by the recurrence
  T_0(b,c)=1, T_1(b,c)=b, and (k+1)T_{k+1}(b,c) = (2k+1)b·T_k(b,c) − k(b^2−4c)·T_{k−1}(b,c) for k≥1,
equivalently T_k(b,c) = ∑_{j=0}^{⌊k/2⌋} \binom{2j}{j}\binom{k}{2j}(b^2-4c)^j ... (the standard polynomials with T_k(2,1) = 2k choose k etc.). For an odd prime p and integer a, let (a/p) denote the Legendre symbol.

Decide whether each of the following four congruences holds.

(i) For every prime p > 3,
  ∑_{k=0}^{p−1} \binom{4k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k} T_k(2702,1) / 384^{2k}
 ≡ { 4x^2 − 2p (mod p^2) if (−1/p) = (p/3) = (p/11) = 1 and p = x^2 + 33y^2;
    2p − 2x^2 (mod p^2) if (−1/p) = 1, (p/3) = (p/11) = −1 and 2p = x^2 + 33y^2;
    12x^2 − 2p (mod p^2) if (p/11) = 1, (−1/p) = (p/3) = −1 and p = 3x^2 + 11y^2;
    2p − 6x^2 (mod p^2) if (p/3) = 1, (−1/p) = (p/11) = −1 and 2p = 3x^2 + 11y^2;
    0 (mod p^2) if (−33/p) = −1 },
and
  ∑_{k=0}^{p−1} (220k + 119) \binom{4k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k} T_k(2702,1) / 384^{2k}
 ≡ p · (•/p) · (55 + 183(−1/p)) (mod p^2)
(with the appropriate Legendre symbol prefactor).

(ii) For every prime p > 3 with p ≠ 7,
  (•/p) ∑_{k=0}^{p−1} \binom{4k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k} T_k(115598,1) / 2688^{2k}
 ≡ { 4x^2 − 2p (mod p^2) if (−1/p) = (p/3) = (p/19) = 1 and p = x^2 + 57y^2;
    2p − 2x^2 (mod p^2) if (−1/p) = 1, (p/3) = (p/19) = −1 and 2p = x^2 + 57y^2;
    12x^2 − 2p (mod p^2) if (p/3) = 1, (−1/p) = (p/19) = −1 and p = 3x^2 + 19y^2;
    2p − 6x^2 (mod p^2) if (p/19) = 1, (−1/p) = (p/3) = −1 and 2p = 3x^2 + 19y^2;
    0 (mod p^2) if (−57/p) = −1 },
and
  ∑_{k=0}^{p−1} (260k + 513) \binom{4k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k} T_k(115598,1) / 2688^{2k}
 ≡ p (21/p)(961 + 65(−1/p)) (mod p^2).

(iii) For every prime p > 3,
  ∑_{n=0}^{p−1} \binom{2n}{n} ∑_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k}\binom{n+2k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k} / 64^k
 ≡ { x^2 − 2p (mod p^2) if (p/3) = (p/17) = 1 and 4p = x^2 + 51y^2;
    3x^2 − 2p (mod p^2) if (p/3) = (p/17) = −1 and 4p = 3x^2 + 17y^2;
    0 (mod p^2) if (p/51) = −1 },
and
  ∑_{n=0}^{p−1} (17n + 9) \binom{2n}{n} ∑_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k}\binom{n+2k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k} / 64^k
 ≡ p((•/p) − 7) (mod p^2).

(iv) For every prime p > 5,
  ∑_{n=0}^{p−1} \binom{2n}{n}/400^n · ∑_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k}\binom{n+2k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k} · 196^{n−k}
 ≡ { x^2 − 2p (mod p^2) if (2/p) = (p/11) = 1 and p = x^2 + 22y^2;
    8x^2 − 2p (mod p^2) if (2/p) = (p/11) = −1 and p = 2x^2 + 11y^2;
    0 (mod p^2) if (−22/p) = −1 },
and
  ∑_{n=0}^{p−1} (33n + 19)/400^n · \binom{2n}{n} · ∑_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k}\binom{n+2k}{2k}\binom{2k}{k} · 196^{n−k}
 ≡ 19p (mod p^2).

Prove or disprove each of (i)–(iv).

Let $N\geq 1$ and let $a_0,a_1,\dots,a_N$ be real numbers. Consider the homogeneous linear iterative functional equation
$$\sum_{j=0}^{N} a_j\,\varphi^{j}(x)=0\qquad\text{for all }x\in(0,\infty),\tag{$\ast$}$$
where $\varphi:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ is the unknown function, $\varphi^{0}(x)=x$, and $\varphi^{j}=\varphi\circ\varphi^{j-1}$ for $j\geq 1$. Associated with $(\ast)$ is the characteristic polynomial
$$P(\lambda)=\sum_{j=0}^{N}a_j\lambda^{j},$$
and let $\Lambda=\{\lambda\in\mathbb{C}:P(\lambda)=0\}$ denote its set of roots. Fix $c\in\Lambda\cap(0,\infty)$, and consider the following three conditions:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $c$ is a simple root of $P$;
\item[(ii)] $(\Lambda\setminus\{c\})\cap(0,\infty)=\emptyset$, i.e., no other positive real number is a root;
\item[(iii)] every $\lambda\in\Lambda\setminus\{c\}$ satisfies $|\lambda|>c$.
\end{itemize}
It is known that conditions (i)–(iii) are sufficient to guarantee that $(\ast)$ admits at most one continuous, monotonic solution $\varphi:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$.

Question: Are conditions (i), (ii), (iii) also necessary for the uniqueness of the continuous monotonic solution $\varphi:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ of $(\ast)$? In other words, if uniqueness of such a solution holds, must each of (i), (ii), (iii) be satisfied?

Let $\mathbb{C}$ denote the complex plane, identified with $\mathbb{R}^2$. Two self-maps $f, g : \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$ are called conjugate via a map $\varphi : \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$ if $\varphi$ is a bijection of $\mathbb{C}$ onto itself and $g = \varphi^{-1} \circ f \circ \varphi$. Give an intrinsic characterization of those self-mappings $f : \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$ which are conjugate to some complex polynomial $P(z) = a_n z^n + a_{n-1} z^{n-1} + \cdots + a_0$ (with $a_i \in \mathbb{C}$, $n \geq 1$) in each of the following three categories of conjugating map $\varphi$:

(a) $\varphi$ is a $C^\infty$-diffeomorphism of $\mathbb{R}^2$;

(b) $\varphi$ is a homeomorphism of $\mathbb{C}$;

(c) $\varphi$ is an arbitrary bijection of $\mathbb{C}$ (no regularity assumed).

In each case, find necessary and sufficient conditions on $f$ for the existence of such a $\varphi$ and a polynomial $P$ with $f = \varphi^{-1} \circ P \circ \varphi$.

Let $\mathbf{N}_0=\{0,1,2,\dots\}$ and let $\omega(\mathbf{N}_0)$ denote the vector space of all scalar sequences $x=(\xi_k)_{k\in\mathbf{N}_0}$. A vector subspace $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbf{N}_0)\subseteq\omega(\mathbf{N}_0)$ equipped with a quasi-norm $\|\cdot\mid\mathfrak{z}\|$ is called a quasi-Banach shift-monotone sequence ideal if:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $(\mathfrak{z}(\mathbf{N}_0),\|\cdot\mid\mathfrak{z}\|)$ is complete;
\item $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbf{N}_0)$ contains the unit sequence $e_0=(1,0,0,\dots)$;
\item whenever $x\in\mathfrak{z}(\mathbf{N}_0)$ and $y=(\eta_k)\in\omega(\mathbf{N}_0)$ satisfies $|\eta_k|\le|\xi_k|$ for all $k$, then $y\in\mathfrak{z}(\mathbf{N}_0)$ with $\|y\mid\mathfrak{z}\|\le\|x\mid\mathfrak{z}\|$;
\item the forward shift $S^{+}:(\xi_0,\xi_1,\dots)\mapsto(0,\xi_0,\xi_1,\dots)$ and the backward shift $S^{-}:(\xi_0,\xi_1,\xi_2,\dots)\mapsto(\xi_1,\xi_2,\dots)$ act as bounded linear operators on $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbf{N}_0)$.
\end{enumerate}
For a bounded linear operator $T$ on $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbf{N}_0)$, denote by $M(T)$ its range. Consider the operator $I-2S^{-}$ acting on $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbf{N}_0)$.

Question: Does there exist a quasi-Banach shift-monotone sequence ideal $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbf{N}_0)$ such that the range $M(I-2S^{-}\mid\mathfrak{z})$ is closed in $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbf{N}_0)$ and has infinite codimension? Equivalently, can this case be ruled out as impossible?

Let $L$ be a fixed finite labeled leaf set with $|L|=n\ge 2$. A discrete time-tree on $L$ is a rooted binary phylogenetic tree with leaf set $L$ together with an assignment of pairwise distinct non-negative integer times to its nodes such that (i) every leaf has time $0$ when required by the model, and (ii) the time of each non-root node is strictly smaller than the time of its parent. Given a discrete time-tree $T$ with internal node times $t_1<t_2<\cdots<t_{n-1}<t_n$ (where $t_n$ is the root time), the event intervals of $T$ are the intervals $I_k=[t_k,t_{k+1}]$ for $k=0,\dots,n-1$ (with $t_0=0$); the length of $I_k$ is $t_{k+1}-t_k$.

Define the following local moves:
- (length move on $I_k$) increase or decrease the length of one event interval $I_k$ by $1$, keeping the lengths of all other intervals the same (and all node times non-negative, distinct, and respecting the parent–child time order);
- (rank move on $I_k$) when $I_k$ has length $1$, swap the times (ranks) of the two internal nodes bounding $I_k$, provided neither is the parent of the other;
- (NNI move on $I_k$) when $I_k$ has length $1$ and the upper endpoint of $I_k$ is the parent of the lower endpoint, perform a nearest-neighbor interchange across the edge connecting them.

Consider the four graphs whose vertex sets are discrete time-trees on $L$ and whose edges correspond to single allowed moves:
- $\mathrm{DtT}^m$: ranked discrete time-trees with leaves all at time $0$, all three move types allowed;
- $\mathrm{DtT}^{um}$: ultrametric variant where leaf times may differ, all three move types allowed;
- $\mathrm{RNNI}$: only rank moves and NNI moves are allowed (every event interval has length $1$);
- $\mathrm{RNNI}^u$: ultrametric variant of $\mathrm{RNNI}$.

For $G\in\{\mathrm{DtT}^m,\mathrm{DtT}^{um},\mathrm{RNNI},\mathrm{RNNI}^u\}$ and two trees $T,R$ in $V(G)$, let $d_G(T,R)$ be the minimum number of edges in a path from $T$ to $R$ in $G$.

Determine the computational complexity of computing $d_G(T,R)$ given $T$ and $R$ as input, for each of the four graphs $G$ above.

Let a tiling T of R^d be a covering of R^d by translates of finitely many compact prototiles whose interiors are pairwise disjoint. The tiling space (continuous hull) X_T is the closure, in the local topology, of the orbit {T - x : x in R^d} of T under translation; X_T is a compact metric space carrying a continuous R^d-action. Two tilings T, T' (or their hulls) are said to be:

(i) bounded-displacement (BD) equivalent if there is a bijection between their tile-sets (equivalently, between associated Delone sets of control points) that moves every point by a uniformly bounded distance;
(ii) bi-Lipschitz (BL) equivalent if there is a bi-Lipschitz bijection between corresponding Delone sets;
(iii) mutually locally derivable (MLD) if each can be obtained from the other by a local rule of finite range;
(iv) topologically conjugate if there is an R^d-equivariant homeomorphism between their hulls;
(v) homeomorphic (as tiling spaces) if their hulls are homeomorphic.

A tiling is linearly repetitive (LR) if there exists C > 0 such that every patch of diameter r occurring in T occurs in every ball of radius Cr in T. Properties such as BD-equivalence to a lattice, BL-equivalence to a lattice, and linear repetitivity are known geometric invariants whose behavior under MLD, topological conjugacy, and homeomorphism has been studied.

Problem. Formulate and study new geometric invariants of tilings (or of their associated Delone sets), distinct from BD-equivalence, BL-equivalence, linear repetitivity, repetitivity, finite local complexity, and existing complexity/repetitivity functions, with the property that each new invariant is preserved under at least one of: mutual local derivability, topological conjugacy of tiling spaces, or homeomorphism of tiling spaces. Establish nontrivial examples distinguishing tilings up to these equivalences, and determine implications between the new invariants and the classical ones.

Consider a heterogeneous computing (HC) environment consisting of a finite suite of machines $M = \{m_1, \dots, m_p\}$ with differing capabilities, interconnected by a communication network with known inter-machine communication costs, on which a workload of tasks (possibly decomposable into communicating subtasks) must be executed. A mapping is an assignment of tasks/subtasks to machines together with a schedule (start times) on each machine. Suppose the environment is subject to a collection of quality-of-service (QoS) requirements, including but not limited to:

(a) priority semantics among tasks or users (a partial or total ordering specifying which jobs preempt or precede others);
(b) bandwidth requirements on inter-machine communication links for specified data transfers;
(c) guaranteed CPU time per unit interval reserved for designated users or task classes;
(d) real-time response constraints, i.e., hard or soft deadlines by which specified tasks must complete.

Develop a formal model of the HC environment, the workload, and the QoS attributes (including security levels and the four classes above), and design mapping and scheduling algorithms that simultaneously incorporate all such QoS components into the mapping/scheduling decision process so as to produce feasible mappings that satisfy every QoS constraint while optimizing a chosen performance objective (e.g., makespan, throughput, or weighted user utility). In particular: (i) provide a mathematical formulation that uniformly expresses heterogeneous QoS components, (ii) characterize the feasibility region of mappings under combined QoS constraints, and (iii) give algorithms (with provable guarantees where possible) for computing such QoS-constrained mappings in static and dynamic settings.

Let M be a smooth (finite-dimensional) manifold, U a nonempty set of admissible control values, and F: M × U → TM a set-valued or single-valued map assigning to each (x,u) ∈ M × U a tangent vector F(x,u) ∈ T_x M. The triple Σ = (M, U, F) is called a general control system; its trajectories are absolutely continuous curves x: I → M satisfying ẋ(t) = F(x(t), u(t)) for some measurable control u: I → U. Let A ⊆ M be a nonempty closed target set.

A feedback law is a map k: M → U (possibly discontinuous, possibly set-valued) such that the closed-loop differential inclusion ẋ(t) ∈ F(x(t), k(x(t))) admits suitably defined solutions (e.g. Carathéodory, Filippov, or sample-and-hold solutions). The set A is said to be:
  • invariant under the closed-loop dynamics if every solution starting in A remains in A for all positive times;
  • a local attractor if there exists a neighborhood N of A such that every solution starting in N converges to A as t → ∞;
  • locally asymptotically stable under the closed-loop dynamics if it is both (Lyapunov) stable and a local attractor.

Problem. Formulate verifiable sufficient conditions on Σ = (M, U, F) and A — for instance in terms of controllability hypotheses, the existence of (nonsmooth) control-Lyapunov functions, or structural properties such as homogeneity — that:
  (a) guarantee the existence of a feedback law k: M → U rendering A invariant for the closed loop;
  (b) guarantee the existence of a feedback law k for which A is a local attractor of the closed-loop dynamics;
  (c) guarantee the existence of a feedback law k that locally asymptotically stabilizes A; and
  (d) are constructive, in the sense that the proof of the sufficient condition yields an explicit procedure for designing such a feedback k, rather than a pure existence statement.

In addition, identify canonical subclasses of general control systems Σ = (M, U, F) (for example, classes determined by regularity of F, structure of U, or geometric properties of M and A) within which conditions of types (a)–(d) can be formulated and proved.

Background. Consider a control system on a smooth manifold M of the form ẋ = f(x,u), where u is a control input, and let A ⊂ M be a compact set that one wishes to render asymptotically stable by means of a (continuous or smooth) feedback law u = k(x). A classical topological obstruction, going back to Milnor and Bhat–Bernstein, asserts that if A is a compact attractor of a continuous flow on M with a basin of attraction that retracts onto A, then A and its basin must have the same Euler characteristic; more generally, retraction-theoretic arguments yield necessary conditions for global or semi-global feedback stabilization expressed in terms of the Euler characteristic χ(A) of the target set. When χ(A) ≠ 0, these methods produce sharp obstructions and, in many situations, matching constructive stabilization results.

Many natural targets, however, have χ(A) = 0. Examples include:
 (a) every closed odd-dimensional manifold (so χ = 0 by Poincaré duality);
 (b) every compact connected Lie group of positive dimension;
 (c) any nontrivial periodic orbit of a flow (a circle).
In such cases retraction-theoretic invariants based on Euler characteristic are silent, and the existing theory does not provide a fine classification of which compact target sets A with χ(A) = 0 can be (locally, semi-globally, or globally) asymptotically stabilized by continuous, smooth, time-varying, or discontinuous feedback, nor does it provide sharp obstructions in this regime.

Problem. Develop a theory of topological and dynamical invariants, going beyond Euler characteristic and pure retraction theory, that:
 1. produces necessary conditions (obstructions) for the existence of feedback stabilizers (continuous, smooth, time-varying, sampled, or discontinuous in a precisely specified sense) of a compact set A ⊂ M with χ(A) = 0 in a control system ẋ = f(x,u);
 2. produces matching sufficient conditions, ideally constructive, in the same regime;
 3. yields a finer classification of compact zero-Euler-characteristic target sets A according to their stabilizability properties, and in particular distinguishes among the classes (a)–(c) above.

Concretely, identify invariants (e.g. coming from K-theory, equivariant cohomology, characteristic classes of normal bundles, Lusternik–Schnirelmann category, vector field indices on bundles, or homotopy-theoretic invariants of pairs (basin, A)) whose vanishing or nonvanishing characterizes the existence of the relevant stabilizing feedback for a given regularity class, and prove the corresponding obstruction/realization theorems.

Let $(\widetilde{M}, J, g)$ be an almost Hermitian manifold of complex dimension $m$, with complex structure $J$ and Hermitian metric $g$. Recall that $\widetilde{M}$ is called a locally conformal Kähler manifold if there exists an open cover $\{U_\alpha\}$ of $\widetilde{M}$ and smooth functions $\sigma_\alpha : U_\alpha \to \mathbb{R}$ such that each local metric $e^{-2\sigma_\alpha} g|_{U_\alpha}$ is Kähler; equivalently, the fundamental 2-form $\Omega(X,Y)=g(X,JY)$ satisfies $d\Omega = \omega \wedge \Omega$ for a closed 1-form $\omega$ (the Lee form). A locally conformal Kähler space form $\widetilde{M}(c)$ is a locally conformal Kähler manifold of constant holomorphic sectional curvature $c$.

A submanifold $M$ of $\widetilde{M}$ is a CR-submanifold if there is a holomorphic distribution $\mathcal{D}$ (i.e. $J\mathcal{D}=\mathcal{D}$) and a totally real distribution $\mathcal{D}^\perp$ (i.e. $J\mathcal{D}^\perp \subset T^\perp M$) such that $TM = \mathcal{D} \oplus \mathcal{D}^\perp$. A CR-warped product submanifold is an isometric immersion of a warped product $M = M_T \times_f M_\perp$, where $M_T$ is a holomorphic submanifold (tangent space $\mathcal{D}$), $M_\perp$ is a totally real submanifold (tangent space $\mathcal{D}^\perp$), $f : M_T \to (0,\infty)$ is a smooth warping function, and the metric on $M$ is $g_{M_T} + f^2 g_{M_\perp}$.

For a unit tangent vector $X \in T_pM$, let $\mathrm{Ric}(X)$ denote the Ricci curvature of $M$ in direction $X$, and let $H$ denote the mean curvature vector of the immersion $M \hookrightarrow \widetilde{M}$.

Problem. Establish a sharp Chen–Ricci type inequality of the form
$$\mathrm{Ric}(X) \le \Phi\!\left(\|H\|^2,\, c,\, \dim M_T,\, \dim M_\perp,\, f,\, \nabla f\right)$$
for every unit tangent vector $X$ at every point of an arbitrary CR-warped product submanifold $M = M_T \times_f M_\perp$ isometrically immersed in:
(i) a locally conformal Kähler space form $\widetilde{M}(c)$;
(ii) the standard sphere $S^{2m}$ equipped with a suitable almost Hermitian (or other) structure of constant holomorphic sectional curvature;
(iii) more generally, any manifold endowed with a structure of constant holomorphic sectional curvature for which CR-warped products are defined.

In each case, explicitly determine the function $\Phi$ and characterize the equality case.

Let A be a commutative ring with unit. For an integer n ≥ 2, an n-th power Büchi sequence in A is a sequence (x_1, x_2, …, x_M) of elements of A such that the second finite difference of the sequence (x_1^n, x_2^n, …, x_M^n) is constant, equal to the value n!·(something natural for n=2 it equals 2; more generally one fixes the constant so that the sequence x_i^n = (a+i)^n satisfies it). The classical Büchi problem B(A) (the case n = 2) asks: does there exist an integer M_0 = M_0(A) such that, for every Büchi sequence (x_1, …, x_M) in A with M ≥ M_0 and second difference of (x_i^2) equal to 2, there exist a, b ∈ A with x_i^2 = (a + i)^2 + b for all i (equivalently, the only long Büchi sequences come from squares of arithmetic progressions, up to a constant). The higher-power analogue B_n(A) asks the same question with squares replaced by n-th powers and the appropriate constant n-th difference.

Question: Fix n ≥ 2. Is it true that for every commutative ring A in the following classes, a positive answer to B(A) implies a positive answer to B_n(A)?

(a) polynomial rings A = R[t] over suitable base rings R;
(b) rational function fields A = K(t);
(c) function fields A of one variable over a field of constants;
(d) rings of meromorphic functions on C and on C_p.

Determine, in each of these settings, whether B(A) ⇒ B_n(A) holds for all n ≥ 2.

Background and setup. Consider second-order elliptic (and more generally convection–diffusion) boundary value problems discretized by hybridizable discontinuous Galerkin (HDG) methods on a conforming simplicial mesh $\mathcal{T}_h$ of a bounded polyhedral domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d$. For each element $K\in\mathcal{T}_h$ an HDG method seeks an approximation $(\boldsymbol{q}_h,u_h,\widehat{u}_h)\in \boldsymbol{V}(K)\times W(K)\times M(\partial K)$ to the flux, the scalar variable, and its trace, where $\boldsymbol{V}(K)$, $W(K)$ are local polynomial spaces and $M(\partial K)$ is a polynomial trace space on the skeleton $\mathcal{E}_h$. Define the numerical trace of the flux on $\partial K$ by
$$\widehat{\boldsymbol{q}}_h\cdot\boldsymbol{n}=\boldsymbol{q}_h\cdot\boldsymbol{n}+\tau\,(u_h-\widehat{u}_h),$$
where $\tau:\partial K\to\mathbb{R}$ is the (possibly face-dependent, possibly tensor-valued) stabilization function. A "mixed method" here means an HDG-style formulation in which the analogous numerical trace is taken with $\tau\equiv 0$ but the local spaces $\boldsymbol{V}(K), W(K), M(\partial K)$ are enriched/modified so that the resulting scheme is well posed and convergent.

Known direction. There is a systematic procedure that, starting from a mixed method (i.e. choices of local spaces with $\tau=0$), produces an equivalent HDG method by introducing a suitable nonzero stabilization $\tau$ while reducing the local spaces; call this the space-to-stabilization transformation.

Problem. Develop a systematic, mathematically rigorous procedure that performs the reverse transformation: given an arbitrary HDG method specified by local spaces $\boldsymbol{V}(K), W(K), M(\partial K)$ and a stabilization function $\tau$, construct enlarged or modified local spaces $\widetilde{\boldsymbol{V}}(K), \widetilde{W}(K), \widetilde{M}(\partial K)$ such that:

(i) the resulting scheme with vanishing stabilization ($\tau\equiv 0$) is well posed and produces, for the same data, an approximation that is at least as accurate (in suitable energy and $L^2$ norms) as the original HDG method;

(ii) the construction is local, explicit, and applies to general element shapes;

(iii) the resulting approximation spaces are genuinely new, i.e. not merely a rewriting of the original scheme in different variables;

(iv) when specialized to the pure convection equation $\boldsymbol{\beta}\cdot\nabla u=f$, the procedure yields a discontinuous Galerkin method that is stable and convergent without any stabilization function.

Either provide such a procedure together with a proof of the above properties, or prove that no such procedure can exist under stated minimal assumptions.

Let $\mathbb{H} = \{a_0 + a_1 i + a_2 j + a_3 k : a_0,a_1,a_2,a_3 \in \mathbb{R}\}$ denote the skew field of real quaternions, with multiplication determined by $i^2=j^2=k^2=ijk=-1$. A quaternion-valued differential equation (QDE) is a system of the form
$$\dot{x}(t) = A(t)\,x(t) + f(t,x(t)), \qquad x(t)\in \mathbb{H}^n,$$
where $A(t)$ is an $n\times n$ quaternion matrix-valued function and $f$ is a quaternion-valued nonlinearity. A discontinuous QDE is a QDE in which the right-hand side, the state, or the control input is allowed to be discontinuous in $t$ or $x$; standard families of such systems include:
(i) impulsive QDEs, in which the state satisfies $\dot x(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t,x(t))$ for $t\ne t_k$ together with jump conditions $x(t_k^+)-x(t_k^-)=I_k(x(t_k^-))$ at a prescribed sequence $t_1<t_2<\cdots$;
(ii) switched QDEs, where the active vector field is selected from a finite family $\{F_\sigma\}_{\sigma\in\Sigma}$ by a switching signal $\sigma:[0,\infty)\to\Sigma$;
(iii) QDEs on time scales, where the derivative is replaced by the delta-derivative on a closed subset $\mathbb{T}\subset\mathbb{R}$;
(iv) piecewise QDEs, where $\mathbb{H}^n$ is partitioned into regions on which the dynamics differ;
(v) fuzzy QDEs, where the dynamics are governed by a Takagi–Sugeno-type rule base with quaternion-valued consequents.

Develop a unified theory of discontinuous QDEs covering classes (i)–(v): in particular, formulate the appropriate notions of solution (e.g.\ Filippov, Carathéodory, or impulsive solutions in the quaternion setting), establish existence, uniqueness, and continuous dependence theorems, and determine whether the discontinuous control strategies (impulsive control, switching control, sliding-mode control, fuzzy control, time-scale control, etc.) used to stabilize, synchronize, or otherwise control discontinuous real- or complex-valued ordinary differential equations can be adapted to discontinuous QDEs, taking into account the noncommutativity of $\mathbb{H}$. Specifically, identify which design methods extend directly, which require genuinely quaternionic modifications, and provide constructive controllers together with quaternion-valued Lyapunov-type stability criteria.

Let V be a finite-dimensional real vector space of dimension n ≥ 2, and let T(V) denote the real vector space of all (0,4)-tensors K : V × V × V × V → R that satisfy the following three identities for all X, Y, Z, W ∈ V:

(i) K(X, Y, Z, W) = −K(Y, X, Z, W);
(ii) K(X, Y, Z, W) + K(Y, Z, X, W) + K(Z, X, Y, W) = 0;
(iii) K(X, Y, Z, W) + K(Y, W, Z, X) + K(W, X, Z, Y) = 0.

(These are the algebraic identities satisfied by the curvature-type tensor K(X, Y, Z, W) := h(R(X, Y)Z, W) associated with a torsion-free affine connection ∇ and a (not necessarily symmetric) bilinear form h on a smooth manifold, where R is the curvature tensor of ∇; note that the usual pair-symmetry K(X,Y,Z,W) = K(Z,W,X,Y) of the Riemannian case is not assumed.)

Determine the algebraic structure of T(V): compute its dimension as a function of n, and decompose T(V) into irreducible components under the natural action of GL(V) (and, in the presence of a non-degenerate symmetric inner product on V, under the corresponding orthogonal group). Describe explicit projections onto each irreducible summand and identify the resulting "Ricci-type" pieces and "Weyl-type" piece analogous to the classical Ricci decomposition of Riemannian curvature tensors.

In the microscopic theory of nuclear large-amplitude collective motion, several distinct prescriptions exist for the collective mass (inertia) tensor associated with a slow collective coordinate q on a self-consistent mean-field manifold:

1. The ASCC (Adiabatic Self-Consistent Collective Coordinate) collective mass, obtained by truncating the collective Hamiltonian at second order in the collective momentum p in a power-series expansion, without requiring the collective kinetic energy to be small compared with intrinsic two-quasiparticle excitation energies.
2. The Inglis–Belyaev cranking mass, obtained from second-order perturbation theory in the time derivative of the mean field.
3. The GCM-GOA mass, obtained from the Generator Coordinate Method in the Gaussian Overlap Approximation.
4. The ATDHFB mass, obtained from the Adiabatic Time-Dependent Hartree–Fock–Bogoliubov expansion to second order in collective velocity.

Provide a systematic, microscopically justified framework that:
(a) Derives all four masses from a common starting point and exhibits the precise approximations that reduce one to another;
(b) Quantifies the discrepancies among them along representative collective paths (e.g., large-amplitude shape vibrations, fission, or shape coexistence) for realistic nuclear Hamiltonians;
(c) Clarifies the role of the differing notions of adiabaticity—in particular reconciling the ASCC adiabaticity (smallness of p, not of collective kinetic energy relative to two-quasiparticle excitations) with the adiabatic-perturbation assumption underlying the cranking formula;
(d) Identifies which mass should be regarded as physically correct, and under what conditions, in the regime where the collective kinetic energy is comparable to two-quasiparticle excitation energies.

Consider a self-bound finite quantum many-fermion system (an atomic nucleus) described by a microscopic Hamiltonian acting on antisymmetric states of A nucleons. Within the time-dependent Hartree–Fock–Bogoliubov (TDHFB) framework, large-amplitude collective motion (LACM) is formulated by seeking a low-dimensional collective submanifold M, embedded in the full TDHFB phase space of generalized Slater determinants, that is approximately decoupled from non-collective degrees of freedom and parametrized by a small set of collective coordinates q and conjugate momenta p. For even-even nuclei this program has been carried out non-perturbatively via the adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate (ASCC) method and related approaches, in which M is determined self-consistently from moving-frame HFB and moving-frame RPA equations without resorting to a small-amplitude expansion around a single equilibrium.

Problem. Develop a non-perturbative microscopic framework that determines such a collective submanifold, and the associated collective Hamiltonian and quantization, for systems containing one or more unpaired quasiparticles — in particular for odd-A nuclei, and more generally for HFB configurations with several blocked quasiparticles — in such a way that:

(i) the framework treats single-particle (quasiparticle) degrees of freedom and collective degrees of freedom on the same footing, allowing strong, finite-amplitude coupling between them rather than perturbative particle–vibration coupling around a fixed mean field;

(ii) it reduces, in the small-amplitude limit and in the limit of vanishing unpaired quasiparticles, to the established quasiparticle-RPA and ASCC descriptions, respectively;

(iii) it yields well-defined moving-frame equations whose solutions specify the collective coordinates, the inertial functions, and the residual coupling to non-collective quasiparticle excitations along the entire collective path, including regions of shape transition, shape coexistence, and other phenomena involving large amplitudes.

Provide such a framework, or prove that a framework satisfying (i)–(iii) exists and is unique under stated self-consistency requirements.

Consider a rotating, self-bound many-fermion system (a model for an atomic nucleus) described by a microscopic Hamiltonian H acting on a many-body Hilbert space, together with a time-dependent mean-field framework in which collective dynamics is parametrized by a few collective coordinates q = (q_1,...,q_n) and conjugate momenta p, derived through the adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate (ASCC) method or equivalent constrained Hartree–Fock–Bogoliubov plus generator-coordinate scheme. Assume the collective potential energy surface V(q; I) (with I the total angular-momentum quantum number generated by cranking about a chosen axis) admits, for sufficiently large I:

(i) a pair of degenerate minima at q = +q_0 and q = -q_0 related by a parity (reflection) operation P, separated by a barrier (these correspond to "left-" and "right-handed" reflection-asymmetric configurations);
(ii) parameter regions in which the barrier between ±q_0 disappears, producing a single minimum at finite octupole deformation (a "transitional" regime toward static reflection-asymmetric shape);
(iii) shallow regions in q-space around configurations of banana (Y_{31}-type triaxial-octupole) or tetrahedral (Y_{32}-type) symmetry.

Problem. Develop, within the microscopic large-amplitude collective motion theory, a quantitative description of the following three phenomena and establish how each depends on the rotational frequency ω (or equivalently on I):

(a) Tunneling splitting ΔE(I) between the symmetric and antisymmetric combinations of the |+q_0⟩ and |−q_0⟩ states as a function of I, including the cranking-induced modification of the inertia tensor M(q;I), the requantized collective Hamiltonian, and Coriolis/time-odd mean-field contributions;
(b) The collective wavefunction and excitation spectrum in the transitional regime where rotation drives the system from a double-well to a single-well octupole-deformed potential, including the I-dependence of the critical control parameter at which the transition occurs;
(c) The large-amplitude zero-point shape-fluctuation dynamics on shallow potential surfaces of banana and tetrahedral type, including computation of the corresponding collective masses, vibrational excitation energies, and electromagnetic transition strengths as functions of I.

Provide a self-consistent derivation, valid beyond small-amplitude RPA, that yields these quantities directly from the underlying microscopic Hamiltonian H.

Consider an odd-odd (or odd-A) atomic nucleus with stable triaxial deformation, in which the total angular momentum vector can be decomposed into three approximately mutually perpendicular components: the collective rotational angular momentum of the triaxial core (aligned with the intermediate principal axis) and the angular momenta of one valence proton (particle) and one valence neutron (hole), aligned with the short and long principal axes, respectively. Such configurations admit two equivalent left-handed and right-handed orientations of the three angular-momentum vectors, related by the chiral operator (a combination of time reversal and a rotation by π). The breaking of this discrete chiral symmetry in the body-fixed frame and its restoration in the laboratory frame produce pairs of nearly degenerate ΔI = 1 rotational bands ("chiral doublets").

Two qualitatively different dynamical regimes are known:
(i) the chiral-vibration regime, in which the system performs slow vibrational motion through the achiral planar configuration, with the splitting between the partner bands governed by zero-point oscillations of the chiral angle;
(ii) the static-chirality regime, in which a sufficiently high barrier between the left- and right-handed minima makes the two configurations long-lived, and the doublet splitting is determined by quantum tunneling through this barrier.

Construct a single theoretical framework for the nuclear many-body problem (specifying the degrees of freedom, the Hamiltonian, and the quantization scheme) that:
(a) reproduces the precursor chiral-vibration regime with its slow-vibration spectrum and electromagnetic transition pattern;
(b) reproduces the static-chirality regime arising from quantum tunneling between the left- and right-handed configurations;
(c) describes, within the same treatment and without switching models, the smooth evolution from regime (i) to regime (ii) as the underlying control parameters (deformation, single-particle alignments, angular momentum) vary.

Develop a computationally tractable microscopic framework that performs full three-dimensional angular-momentum projection on top of triaxially deformed relativistic mean-field (or analogous nuclear energy-density-functional) reference states, including configuration mixing through the generator coordinate method and the explicit treatment of one- and multi-quasiparticle excitations, and apply it to odd-A and odd-odd chiral-doublet candidate nuclei (such as those in the A ≈ 100, 130, and 190 mass regions). Specifically, design and demonstrate algorithms that:

1. Generate a sufficient set of triaxially deformed quasiparticle vacua and blocked one- and two-quasiparticle configurations spanning the relevant valence space for an odd-mass or odd-odd chiral candidate;
2. Perform three-dimensional angular-momentum projection P^J_{MK} on these quasiparticle states, yielding projected states of good total angular momentum J;
3. Mix the projected configurations (configuration mixing / GCM with triaxial deformation parameters β, γ as generator coordinates) to obtain energy spectra, electromagnetic transition matrix elements (B(M1), B(E2)), and the predicted near-degenerate chiral partner bands;
4. Control the combinatorial growth of the configuration space and the cost of the Pfaffian/overlap evaluations sufficiently to make the full calculation feasible on currently available computational resources, and benchmark the predictions against experimental chiral-doublet candidates rather than only against light even-even test cases such as ²⁴Mg.

Show whether such a fully self-consistent, beyond-mean-field calculation can reproduce the observed level schemes and transition strengths of established chiral-doublet candidates.

A compact hyperkähler manifold is a compact, simply connected Kähler manifold $M$ such that the space $H^0(M,\Omega^2_M)$ of global holomorphic 2-forms is spanned by an everywhere non-degenerate holomorphic symplectic form $\sigma$. Equivalently, $M$ admits a Riemannian metric whose holonomy group equals $\mathrm{Sp}(n)$, where $\dim_{\mathbb{C}} M = 2n$.

Up to deformation equivalence, the currently known examples of compact hyperkähler manifolds fall into the following families:
(i) K3 surfaces (dimension 2);
(ii) Hilbert schemes $\mathrm{Hilb}^n(S)$ of $n$ points on a K3 surface $S$, for $n \geq 2$ (dimension $2n$);
(iii) generalized Kummer varieties $K_n(A)$ associated with a complex torus $A$ of dimension 2, for $n \geq 2$ (dimension $2n$);
(iv) the two sporadic examples $\mathrm{OG}_6$ and $\mathrm{OG}_{10}$ of O'Grady, in complex dimensions 6 and 10 respectively.

Problem: Construct a compact hyperkähler manifold that is not deformation equivalent to any member of the four families (i)–(iv) listed above.

Consider a low-density nuclear system consisting of protons and neutrons interacting through a realistic nucleon–nucleon potential, in which four-nucleon (spin–isospin saturated) bound clusters known as α particles can form. At sufficiently low density and temperature, in-medium four-body correlations are expected to give rise to a quartet (α-particle) Bose–Einstein-type quantum condensate, while at higher densities the increasing Pauli blocking by the surrounding nucleonic medium destroys these clusters and the condensate disappears. A standard starting point treats the medium as uncorrelated (ordinary mean field), but this neglects correlations of the medium itself, which becomes essential when the bulk system is "α matter" (a fluid built from α-like clusters).

Formulate a cluster mean-field approximation for this system in which the medium acting on a chosen four-nucleon cluster is itself built self-consistently from correlated four-body (α-like) clusters rather than from uncorrelated nucleons, and use it to derive:

(a) an in-medium four-nucleon (Faddeev–Yakubovsky-type) equation for the quartet bound-state wave function and chemical potential, with single-nucleon occupation numbers and self-energies determined self-consistently by the α-cluster content of the medium;

(b) a gap-like (quartet condensate) equation determining the critical temperature and density for the onset of α-particle quantum condensation, reducing in the dilute limit to Bose–Einstein condensation of α particles and incorporating the Mott-like dissolution of the quartet bound state with increasing density;

(c) a quantitative description, within this framework, of the density region in which the quartet condensate exists and the mechanism by which it disappears as the density approaches nuclear saturation density.

The problem is to construct such an improved cluster mean-field scheme that consistently includes α matter as the background medium and to use it to describe both the formation and the high-density disappearance of the quartet condensate.

In low-energy antikaon–nucleon physics, consider the isospin I=0, S-wave (orbital angular momentum L=0) K⁻p quasi-bound state in the strangeness S=−1 meson–baryon coupled-channel system (with channels including K̄N and Σπ). Determine:

(a) the precise pole position(s) (mass and width) of this quasi-bound state in the complex energy plane: in particular, whether there is a single pole consistent with the traditional Λ(1405) near 1405 MeV/c², or whether the amplitude possesses a two-pole structure with a higher-mass pole near 1420 MeV/c² in addition to a broader lower-mass pole;

(b) the strength of the s-wave I=0 K̄N interaction implied by the location of this state;

(c) which Σπ invariant-mass measurements (for example, stopped K⁻ on light nuclei such as ⁴He or deuterium, producing Σπ final states) can decisively discriminate between the single-pole and two-pole hypotheses and pin down the mass, width, and pole content of the I=0, L=0 K̄N quasi-bound state.

Consider a two-dimensional electron system (e.g., electrons confined to an interface or quantum well in a semiconductor heterostructure) at low temperature, characterized by carrier density n, effective mass m*, Landé g factor g*, and Pauli spin susceptibility χ ∝ g* m*. As n is decreased toward a critical density n_c at which a metal-insulator transition occurs, experiments find that:

(i) m*/m_b grows strongly (by factors of order three or more) and appears to diverge as n → n_c, where m_b is the band mass;
(ii) χ likewise grows strongly and appears to diverge;
(iii) g* remains nearly unchanged, close to its bulk value, so the enhancement of χ is carried almost entirely by m*;
(iv) the enhancement is observed not only in the diffusive transport regime Tτ/ℏ ≪ 1 (where τ is the elastic scattering time) but also deep in the ballistic regime Tτ/ℏ ≫ 1;
(v) the effect appears universal across silicon-based 2D systems with widely different disorder potentials.

Provide a controlled microscopic many-body theory of the interacting 2D electron liquid that:
(a) predicts a strong enhancement, and possible divergence, of m* and χ as n → n_c from the metallic side;
(b) yields a g factor that remains essentially uncorrected;
(c) is valid in the ballistic regime Tτ/ℏ ≫ 1, not only in the diffusive regime;
(d) is independent of the specific disorder realization, in agreement with the observed universality.

In particular, decide whether the divergence of m* arises from a flattening of the single-particle spectrum at the Fermi energy (a fermion-condensation / Khodel–Shaginyan type scenario) or from another mechanism, and derive the resulting density dependence of m*(n) and χ(n) near n_c.

Background. Let $X$ be a smooth projective Calabi–Yau threefold and let $M$ be a moduli space of stable coherent sheaves $E$ on $X$. The classical Donaldson–Thomas invariants of $X$ are defined as integrals against a virtual fundamental class on $M$, whose construction relies on a perfect obstruction theory in which infinitesimal deformations are governed by $\operatorname{Ext}^1(E,E)$ and obstructions by $\operatorname{Ext}^2(E,E)$, together with the (essentially) vanishing condition $\operatorname{Ext}^i(E,E)=0$ for $i>2$. This vanishing fails on smooth projective varieties of dimension $n>3$, where higher Ext groups $\operatorname{Ext}^i(E,E)$ for $i\ge 3$ are generally nonzero, and a standard perfect obstruction theory of the above type does not exist on moduli of sheaves.

Problem. Construct a theory of Donaldson–Thomas–type enumerative invariants for moduli spaces of stable coherent sheaves on smooth projective varieties $X$ of complex dimension strictly greater than $3$. More precisely, give a general definition of integer- (or rational-) valued invariants attached to such moduli spaces that:

1. extends, in a precise sense, the classical Donaldson–Thomas invariants of Calabi–Yau threefolds when $\dim X = 3$;
2. produces deformation-invariant numerical counts of sheaves on $X$ when $\dim X > 3$, despite the failure of the vanishing $\operatorname{Ext}^i(E,E)=0$ for $i>2$;
3. is accompanied by a construction of a (virtual) cycle on the moduli space against which natural cohomology classes can be integrated to yield these invariants.

Formulate and establish such a definition (and the corresponding virtual class construction) in dimension $>3$, or prove that no such definition with the listed properties can exist.

Consider a coherent polycrystalline solid modeled in a phase-field framework, in which the local crystallographic state is described by a (scalar or quaternion-valued) orientation field θ(x,t), coupled to a structural order parameter φ(x,t) that distinguishes solid and liquid (or that resolves the underlying periodic atomic density, as in phase-field-crystal models). In the coherent regime, neighboring crystalline regions remain connected through their lattice, so that local rotations of θ are not independent degrees of freedom but are kinematically tied to the dislocation density tensor α = ∇ × β^p, where β^p is the plastic distortion: changes of orientation across a region are accommodated by the nucleation, motion, and rearrangement of dislocations rather than by a free local relaxation.

Standard phase-field models of polycrystals postulate a purely relaxational law of the form ∂_t θ = −M δF/δθ for some free-energy functional F[φ,θ] and mobility M, which does not respect this kinematic constraint.

Problem. Derive a tractable, phase-field-compatible evolution equation for the orientation field θ(x,t) in coherent crystalline matter that:
(i) is obtained from, and consistent with, an evolution equation for the dislocation density tensor α (so that ∂_t θ is determined by dislocation transport and reactions, not by independent local relaxation);
(ii) incorporates the long-range elastic interactions between dislocations through the elastic stress field σ generated by α and an applied external strain, with dislocation velocity driven by the Peach–Koehler force;
(iii) accounts for interactions with other defects (grain boundaries, additional phases, free surfaces) represented in the phase-field setting;
(iv) reduces, in suitable limits, to known continuum descriptions of grain-boundary motion and to standard relaxational orientation dynamics where appropriate;
(v) is closed and computationally tractable as a coupled PDE system for (φ, θ, α) (or an equivalent set of fields).

Formulate such an equation and establish its thermodynamic consistency and well-posedness as a model for orientation dynamics in coherent polycrystals.

For a field $F$, let $\mathrm{Br}(F)$ denote its Brauer group. For each class $\alpha \in \mathrm{Br}(F)$, let $\mathrm{per}(\alpha)$ denote its period (its order in $\mathrm{Br}(F)$) and $\mathrm{ind}(\alpha)$ its index (the degree of the unique division algebra representing $\alpha$). Define the Brauer dimension $\mathrm{Br.dim}(F)$ to be the smallest non-negative integer $d$ such that for every finitely generated extension $E/F$ of transcendence degree $\leq 1$ and every $\alpha \in \mathrm{Br}(E)$, one has $\mathrm{ind}(\alpha) \mid \mathrm{per}(\alpha)^d$ (and $+\infty$ if no such $d$ exists).

Problem: Identify a useful class $\mathcal{C}$ of fields and define a numerical invariant $\dim F \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$ for $F \in \mathcal{C}$ such that the following two properties hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathrm{Br.dim}(F) \leq \dim F - 1$ for every $F \in \mathcal{C}$;
\item $\dim(F(t)) = \dim F + 1$ for every $F \in \mathcal{C}$ (where $F(t)$ is the rational function field in one variable, which should also lie in $\mathcal{C}$).
\end{enumerate}

Let $F$ be an infinite field and let $n\geq 2$. Consider $m\geq 2$ generic $n\times n$ matrices $X_1,\dots,X_m$, where each $X_k=(x_{ij}^{(k)})$ has entries that are independent commuting indeterminates over $F$. Let $F\{X_1,\dots,X_m\}\subset M_n(F[x_{ij}^{(k)}])$ be the (noncommutative) $F$-subalgebra generated by these matrices, called the ring of generic $n\times n$ matrices. Its center is a commutative integral domain whose field of fractions is denoted $Z_m(F,n)$. Write $Z(F,n):=Z_2(F,n)$. Let $Z_H(F,n)$ denote the center of the corresponding generic division algebra obtained by inverting the nonzero central elements (equivalently, the center of the universal division algebra of $n\times n$ generic matrices).

Recall that a field extension $K/F$ is called:
 (i) rational if $K\cong F(t_1,\dots,t_r)$ is purely transcendental over $F$;
 (ii) stably rational if $K(s_1,\dots,s_\ell)$ is rational over $F$ for some independent indeterminates $s_1,\dots,s_\ell$;
 (iii) retract rational if there exist a localization $A$ of a polynomial ring $F[y_1,\dots,y_N]$ with $K\cong\operatorname{Frac}(A)$ and $F$-algebra homomorphisms $A\xrightarrow{\varphi} F[[t]][t^{-1}]$ and $F[[t]][t^{-1}]$-style retract realizing the identity (equivalently, every generic splitting can be retracted).

Determine, for general $n$ and $m$, whether each of the field extensions $Z(F,n)/F$, $Z_m(F,n)/F$, and $Z_H(F,n)/F$ is rational, stably rational, or retract rational over $F$.

Let $F$ be a field and let $G$ be a smooth linear algebraic group over $F$. Recall that the essential dimension $\mathrm{ed}_F(G)$ is defined as the minimum, over all field extensions $K/F$ and all $G$-torsors $T$ over $K$, of the maximum, over $T$, of the minimum transcendence degree $\operatorname{trdeg}_F(K_0)$ such that $T$ descends to a $G$-torsor over an intermediate subfield $F\subseteq K_0\subseteq K$. For a positive integer $n$ and a positive integer $m$ dividing $n$, let $\mu_m\subset GL_n$ denote the central subgroup of $m$-th roots of unity (scalar matrices), so that $GL_n/\mu_m$ is a connected reductive group whose torsors over a field $K$ classify central simple $K$-algebras of degree $n$ whose Brauer class has exponent dividing $m$.

Determine the exact values of:
1. $\mathrm{ed}_F(GL_8/\mu_2)$ when $F$ is a field of characteristic $2$;
2. $\mathrm{ed}_F(GL_{16}/\mu_2)$ when $F$ is a field of characteristic different from $2$;
3. $\mathrm{ed}_F(GL_{p^2}/\mu_p)$ for every odd prime $p$ and every field $F$.

Let $F$ be a field and let $\operatorname{Br}(F)$ denote its Brauer group, i.e. the group of equivalence classes of central simple $F$-algebras under the tensor product, equivalently $H^2(\operatorname{Gal}(F^{\mathrm{sep}}/F), (F^{\mathrm{sep}})^\times)$. For a prime $p$, let $\operatorname{Br}(F)_p$ denote the $p$-primary torsion subgroup of $\operatorname{Br}(F)$, i.e. the subgroup of elements whose order is a power of $p$. Recall that an abelian group $A$ is divisible if $nA = A$ for every positive integer $n$, and is an elementary abelian $2$-group if $2A = 0$.

Prove or disprove: for every field $F$ and every prime $p$, at least one of the following holds:
(a) $\operatorname{Br}(F)_p = 0$;
(b) $\operatorname{Br}(F)_p$ contains a nonzero divisible subgroup;
(c) $p = 2$ and $\operatorname{Br}(F)_2$ is an elementary abelian $2$-group.

Consider the leptonic decay $B^+ \to \tau^+ \nu_\tau$ measured at a future high-luminosity $e^+e^-$ $B$-factory operating at the $\Upsilon(4S)$ resonance, in which one $B$ meson of the $B\bar B$ pair is fully reconstructed in a hadronic or semileptonic mode (the "$B_{\rm tag}$") and the signal $B^+ \to \tau^+ \nu_\tau$ decay (the "$B_{\rm sig}$") is reconstructed on the recoil. Let $\mathcal{B} \equiv \mathrm{BR}(B^+ \to \tau^+ \nu_\tau)$ and assume:

(i) the irreducible relative systematic uncertainty on $\mathcal{B}$, dominated by the $B_{\rm tag}$ and $B_{\rm sig}$ reconstruction efficiencies and by $B\bar B$ counting, currently amounts to $\sigma_{\rm sys}/\mathcal{B} \approx 8.7\%$;
(ii) the relative statistical uncertainty scales as $\sigma_{\rm stat}/\mathcal{B} \propto 1/\sqrt{L}$ where $L$ is the integrated luminosity, with $\sigma_{\rm stat}/\mathcal{B} \approx 2 \cdot 8.7\% = 17.4\%$ at the present reference luminosity;
(iii) the total relative uncertainty is $\sigma_{\rm tot}/\mathcal{B} = \sqrt{(\sigma_{\rm stat}/\mathcal{B})^2 + (\sigma_{\rm sys}/\mathcal{B})^2}$.

Question: Is it achievable, by improvements in the analysis techniques used to determine the $B_{\rm tag}$ and $B_{\rm sig}$ reconstruction efficiencies and the $B\bar B$ counting, to reduce the irreducible systematic uncertainty $\sigma_{\rm sys}/\mathcal{B}$ by a factor of two (to roughly $4.35\%$) so that, at an integrated luminosity of $L = 75\,\mathrm{ab}^{-1}$, the measurement is not dominated by systematics and the total uncertainty $\sigma_{\rm tot}/\mathcal{B}$ reaches approximately $4.5\%$?
